WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:09.790 --> 00:00:11.340
- Hello, hello everyone.

00:00:11.340 --> 00:00:12.910
Welcome and thank you so much for coming.

00:00:12.910 --> 00:00:13.890
I'm Stephen Pitti.

00:00:13.890 --> 00:00:16.410
I'm a professor of history
in American Studies

00:00:16.410 --> 00:00:18.330
and ethnicity, race, and migration,

00:00:18.330 --> 00:00:20.090
and the director of the
Center for the Study

00:00:20.090 --> 00:00:22.840
of Race, Indigeneity, and
Transnational Migration.

00:00:22.840 --> 00:00:26.180
It's great to see everyone
here today on Election Day.

00:00:26.180 --> 00:00:28.973
Hope you all, those of
you who can, have voted.

00:00:29.810 --> 00:00:34.020
Really, really thrilled
to welcome back to campus

00:00:34.020 --> 00:00:36.040
Monica Muñoz Martinez,

00:00:36.040 --> 00:00:38.370
the Stanley J. Bernstein
assistant professor

00:00:38.370 --> 00:00:41.470
of American Studies at Brown University.

00:00:41.470 --> 00:00:46.240
Monica was named last year
an Andrew Carnegie Fellow

00:00:46.240 --> 00:00:50.950
as one of, quote, the country's
most creative thinkers,

00:00:50.950 --> 00:00:53.540
undertaking quote, research on challenges

00:00:53.540 --> 00:00:56.390
to democracy and international order.

00:00:56.390 --> 00:00:58.980
A major honor and a
certainly a fitting one,

00:00:58.980 --> 00:01:00.920
given the work that she does.

00:01:00.920 --> 00:01:05.270
Monica received her PhD in
American Studies here at Yale,

00:01:05.270 --> 00:01:09.540
and at Brown, she offers
courses on Latinx studies,

00:01:09.540 --> 00:01:12.140
immigration, histories of violence,

00:01:12.140 --> 00:01:13.840
histories of policing,

00:01:13.840 --> 00:01:16.870
and public memory in
United States history.

00:01:16.870 --> 00:01:21.300
Her remarkable first book, The
Injustice Never Leaves You:

00:01:21.300 --> 00:01:24.890
Anti-Mexican Violence in the United States

00:01:24.890 --> 00:01:27.710
was just published by
Harvard University Press

00:01:27.710 --> 00:01:29.710
in September of 2018.

00:01:29.710 --> 00:01:31.290
Recommend it to all of you.

00:01:31.290 --> 00:01:32.410
There are a number of students in the room

00:01:32.410 --> 00:01:34.210
who already had the chance to read it.

00:01:34.210 --> 00:01:36.853
And she'll be discussing
that project here today.

00:01:37.870 --> 00:01:41.150
Monica Martinez is also
the primary investigator

00:01:41.150 --> 00:01:44.740
for Mapping Violence, a
digital project at Brown

00:01:44.740 --> 00:01:48.670
that documents histories of
racial violence in Texas.

00:01:48.670 --> 00:01:50.730
She's also a founding member

00:01:51.604 --> 00:01:55.960
of the nonprofit organization
Refusing to Forget,

00:01:55.960 --> 00:01:59.200
an organization that calls
for a public reckoning

00:01:59.200 --> 00:02:01.460
with racial violence in Texas.

00:02:01.460 --> 00:02:05.400
That team developed an
award-winning exhibit

00:02:05.400 --> 00:02:08.910
for the Bullock Texas State History Museum

00:02:08.910 --> 00:02:12.150
that marked the first
time in Texas history

00:02:12.150 --> 00:02:15.230
that a cultural institution acknowledged

00:02:15.230 --> 00:02:17.550
Texas's state responsibility

00:02:17.550 --> 00:02:21.670
for a period of racial
terror in the 20th century.

00:02:21.670 --> 00:02:25.770
Monica has also helped to
secure state historical markers

00:02:25.770 --> 00:02:28.100
along the US-Mexico border,

00:02:28.100 --> 00:02:30.470
commemorating racial violence in Texas,

00:02:30.470 --> 00:02:32.300
and that has kept her very busy.

00:02:32.300 --> 00:02:35.940
Indeed up until just hours
ago, you were busy with this,

00:02:35.940 --> 00:02:39.010
with a ceremonial unveiling,
and other types of work.

00:02:39.010 --> 00:02:42.538
Please join me in welcoming
Monica Martinez back to Yale.

00:02:42.538 --> 00:02:45.705
(audience applauding)

00:02:47.200 --> 00:02:49.030
