00:05:00.960 --> 00:05:05.960
and share a bit with you from the book.

00:05:06.330 --> 00:05:10.600
I want to give a quick overview

00:05:10.600 --> 00:05:14.250
of just some of the interventions
that the book makes,

00:05:14.250 --> 00:05:16.190
and that I think has helped

00:05:16.190 --> 00:05:19.260
lead to the reception the good reception

00:05:19.260 --> 00:05:20.670
that the book has received.

00:05:20.670 --> 00:05:24.910
So to give people just a broader
sense of the interventions,

00:05:24.910 --> 00:05:26.810
I'll actually read a section of the book

00:05:26.810 --> 00:05:30.680
to help introduce some of
the, a quick case study,

00:05:30.680 --> 00:05:33.110
and to also give people
a sense of the methods

00:05:33.110 --> 00:05:35.040
and the questions that
I engage in the book.

00:05:35.040 --> 00:05:37.570
And then after that,
I'm gonna talk through

00:05:37.570 --> 00:05:39.510
some of the problems and challenges

00:05:39.510 --> 00:05:42.310
with actually bringing
about a public reckoning

00:05:42.310 --> 00:05:45.203
and dialogue about this
history of racial violence.

00:05:46.150 --> 00:05:47.500
So I also, can I get a sense

00:05:47.500 --> 00:05:50.163
of how many people in the
room have read the book?

00:05:51.540 --> 00:05:52.630
That's a good number, okay.

00:05:52.630 --> 00:05:55.330
And how many of you know nothing about

00:05:55.330 --> 00:05:57.833
history of anti-Mexican violence in Texas?

00:05:59.120 --> 00:06:01.530
Okay, alright, so we've
got a range of audience.

00:06:01.530 --> 00:06:03.710
I just wanted to get a sense.

00:06:03.710 --> 00:06:07.040
So just very broadly,

00:06:07.040 --> 00:06:08.970
my book The Injustice Never Leaves You

00:06:08.970 --> 00:06:11.240
grapples with a period
of anti-Mexican violence

00:06:11.240 --> 00:06:13.270
in Texas in the early 20th century.

00:06:13.270 --> 00:06:16.240
it was a period between 1910 and 1920

00:06:16.240 --> 00:06:19.383
that was a reign of
terror, state sanctioned.

00:06:20.937 --> 00:06:23.070
It was a reign of terror

00:06:23.070 --> 00:06:28.070
enacted by a coalition of Texas Rangers,

00:06:28.520 --> 00:06:31.270
the state police officers in Texas,

00:06:31.270 --> 00:06:33.370
local law enforcement officers,

00:06:33.370 --> 00:06:36.240
Anglo vigilantes, and US soldiers.

00:06:36.240 --> 00:06:39.650
And so it's striking,
the parallels that I see

00:06:39.650 --> 00:06:41.540
from 100 years ago and today,

00:06:41.540 --> 00:06:43.880
in terms of the culture
of anti-Mexican violence.

00:06:43.880 --> 00:06:45.740
So in the early 20th century,

00:06:45.740 --> 00:06:50.740
there was a, politicians, the
media, and local residents

00:06:52.290 --> 00:06:55.500
criminalized ethnic Mexicans as bandits,

00:06:55.500 --> 00:06:58.150
as threats to the nation,

00:06:58.150 --> 00:07:01.020
as threats to Anglos more specifically,

00:07:01.020 --> 00:07:03.893
and also as threats to Anglo capitalism.

00:07:05.150 --> 00:07:07.473
And so you saw a period of,

00:07:08.852 --> 00:07:11.110
in which there were calls to increase

00:07:11.110 --> 00:07:13.563
the police presence on the border.

00:07:14.520 --> 00:07:19.040
In 1914, the government
responded by sending

00:07:19.040 --> 00:07:21.530
over 100,000 US troops to the border.

00:07:21.530 --> 00:07:26.520
And so the criminalization of
ethnic Mexicans in this period

00:07:26.520 --> 00:07:29.240
was answered by the brutal policing

00:07:29.240 --> 00:07:31.080
of this intersection regime

00:07:31.080 --> 00:07:33.520
of law enforcement officers, soldiers,

00:07:33.520 --> 00:07:36.220
and Anglo vigilantes.

00:07:36.220 --> 00:07:39.780
And one of the tensions in the book

00:07:39.780 --> 00:07:42.070
is that in this period

00:07:42.070 --> 00:07:45.010
there was an effort to
justify the violence,

00:07:45.010 --> 00:07:50.010
and that led to public
understandings in this period

00:07:51.160 --> 00:07:54.530
of anti-Mexican violence
actually being progress.

00:07:54.530 --> 00:07:56.650
And so one of the tensions in the book

00:07:56.650 --> 00:07:58.083
is that on the one hand,

00:07:58.940 --> 00:08:01.650
there have been calls for over 100 years

00:08:01.650 --> 00:08:03.170
by people who live in the borderlands,

00:08:03.170 --> 00:08:06.630
people from this era, who sought justice,

00:08:06.630 --> 00:08:09.700
and from generation to
generation passed down an effort

00:08:09.700 --> 00:08:13.130
to try to change public understandings

00:08:13.130 --> 00:08:15.490
of this period of racial violence.

00:08:15.490 --> 00:08:18.350
But that's in tension with
a broad national forgetting

00:08:18.350 --> 00:08:20.557
of this period of anti-Mexican violence.

00:08:20.557 --> 00:08:24.030
And so it's estimated
that between 1910 and 1920

00:08:24.030 --> 00:08:25.590
that hundreds of ethnic Mexicans

00:08:25.590 --> 00:08:28.250
were murdered by these agents,

00:08:28.250 --> 00:08:30.970
and that those victims included

00:08:32.250 --> 00:08:34.390
American citizens of Mexican descent,

00:08:34.390 --> 00:08:37.180
people that were racialized as Mexican,

00:08:37.180 --> 00:08:39.910
as foreign others, as criminal threats,

00:08:39.910 --> 00:08:41.340
and Mexican nationals

00:08:41.340 --> 00:08:44.450
that were living in
Texas during this period.

00:08:44.450 --> 00:08:46.190
And so a good number of the people

00:08:46.190 --> 00:08:50.120
that had moved into South Texas
in this period were refugees

00:08:50.120 --> 00:08:53.950
fleeing the violence of
the Mexican Revolution.

00:08:53.950 --> 00:08:57.343
And so in this period,

00:08:59.010 --> 00:09:01.730
most people publicly have forgotten

00:09:01.730 --> 00:09:06.730
these calls for violence, and
this period of racial terror.

00:09:08.233 --> 00:09:10.300
And one of the interventions
that I make in the book

00:09:10.300 --> 00:09:12.820
is not only to document
the kinds of violence,

00:09:12.820 --> 00:09:14.290
the widespread violence,

00:09:14.290 --> 00:09:18.520
but also to document the ways in which

00:09:18.520 --> 00:09:21.000
this kind of violence
was institutionalized.

00:09:21.000 --> 00:09:24.310
And so for historians of the borderlands,

00:09:24.310 --> 00:09:25.640
other historians that have

00:09:25.640 --> 00:09:27.520
written about this period of violence,

00:09:27.520 --> 00:09:30.320
there's a long tradition of historians,

00:09:30.320 --> 00:09:32.620
starting from the early 20th century

00:09:32.620 --> 00:09:34.260
with Walter Prescott Webb,

00:09:34.260 --> 00:09:38.280
that really created and institutionalized

00:09:38.280 --> 00:09:39.850
not only in the field of history,

00:09:39.850 --> 00:09:44.170
but also in public history,
a progress narrative.

00:09:44.170 --> 00:09:47.280
That Mexicans are a
violent group of people,

00:09:47.280 --> 00:09:49.280
that they are racially inferior,

00:09:49.280 --> 00:09:54.150
that they have dirty water,
trough water for blood,

00:09:54.150 --> 00:09:56.640
and that Texas Ranger brutality

00:09:56.640 --> 00:09:59.090
at the hands of Texas Rangers
of state police officers

00:09:59.090 --> 00:10:01.203
was actually progress.

00:10:02.080 --> 00:10:04.320
It secured the border for Anglo capital,

00:10:04.320 --> 00:10:06.433
and for Anglo Americans.

00:10:07.300 --> 00:10:09.260
But one of the ways in which I think

00:10:09.260 --> 00:10:10.770
that the borderlands historians

00:10:10.770 --> 00:10:12.230
that have returned to this period

00:10:12.230 --> 00:10:14.030
to actually expose the brutality

00:10:14.030 --> 00:10:16.720
and expose the murders of people,

00:10:16.720 --> 00:10:19.400
the denial of due process

00:10:19.400 --> 00:10:21.260
for ethnic Mexicans in this region

00:10:21.260 --> 00:10:24.250
is that historians have
also fallen into this trap

00:10:24.250 --> 00:10:27.670
of looking at this
period as ending in 1920.

00:10:27.670 --> 00:10:31.010
And so in 1919, J.T. Canales,

00:10:31.010 --> 00:10:33.320
who was a state representative
from Brownsville,

00:10:33.320 --> 00:10:36.440
he helped to lead an investigation
into the Texas Rangers.

00:10:36.440 --> 00:10:41.147
And so some of the acts of
brutality become so egregious,

00:10:42.270 --> 00:10:46.690
namely the Porvenir
massacre in January 1918.

00:10:46.690 --> 00:10:50.280
It was the the massacre of 15 men and boys

00:10:50.280 --> 00:10:52.713
who were in the custody of Texas Rangers.

00:10:53.910 --> 00:10:57.480
This community was interrupted
by a group of Texas Rangers,

00:10:57.480 --> 00:11:00.200
local ranching family, local ranchers,

00:11:00.200 --> 00:11:03.890
and they were supported
by a US cavalry troop.

00:11:03.890 --> 00:11:05.430
And in the middle of the night,

00:11:05.430 --> 00:11:08.690
this community was awoken,
taken from their homes,

00:11:08.690 --> 00:11:12.070
and this group of 15 men
and boys was executed.

00:11:12.070 --> 00:11:16.670
And so that event caused,
sort of put a spotlight

00:11:16.670 --> 00:11:17.900
on the kinds of brutality

00:11:17.900 --> 00:11:20.230
that had been occurring over the decade.

00:11:20.230 --> 00:11:22.220
And in 1919, there was an investigation

00:11:22.220 --> 00:11:23.120
into the Texas Rangers,

00:11:23.120 --> 00:11:26.653
which led to weeks of investigations.

00:11:27.990 --> 00:11:30.190
But as a result of the crimes

00:11:30.190 --> 00:11:34.090
that were brought to light
from that investigation,

00:11:34.090 --> 00:11:36.390
the Texas Ranger force
was reduced in number.

00:11:36.390 --> 00:11:39.930
So part of the escalation
of policing in this period

00:11:39.930 --> 00:11:42.730
took the form of the the
Texas state police force

00:11:42.730 --> 00:11:45.040
growing dramatically from under

00:11:45.040 --> 00:11:49.200
less than 100 Texas Rangers in 1914,

00:11:49.200 --> 00:11:54.200
by 1919 there was 1,350
Texas Rangers approximately.

00:11:54.640 --> 00:11:57.160
And so one of the ways
that the state responded

00:11:57.160 --> 00:12:02.120
to these kinds of acts of
brutality being exposed

00:12:02.120 --> 00:12:05.810
was to reduce the numbers of
the Texas Rangers drastically.

00:12:05.810 --> 00:12:07.680
And so they reduced the force

00:12:07.680 --> 00:12:11.373
from over 1,300 to less than 75.

00:12:12.270 --> 00:12:14.070
And so even for historians

00:12:14.070 --> 00:12:15.270
that have returned to this period

00:12:15.270 --> 00:12:17.160
to recover in this period of violence,

00:12:17.160 --> 00:12:19.940
they've sort of understood
the Canales Investigation

00:12:19.940 --> 00:12:21.830
as leading to the end,

00:12:21.830 --> 00:12:24.400
the decline of this kind of brutality.

00:12:24.400 --> 00:12:26.480
And so part of the work that I've done

00:12:26.480 --> 00:12:29.650
is to trace the movement

00:12:29.650 --> 00:12:32.390
of some of these Texas
Rangers and police officers

00:12:32.390 --> 00:12:33.513
to track the kinds of violence

00:12:33.513 --> 00:12:37.200
that they participated in
different parts of the state.

00:12:37.200 --> 00:12:42.030
But also to note that and
to highlight quite clearly

00:12:42.030 --> 00:12:45.050
that firing somebody from
the Texas Ranger Police Force

00:12:45.050 --> 00:12:47.250
is not justice for murder,

00:12:47.250 --> 00:12:50.210
and that in fact the Texas Rangers,

00:12:50.210 --> 00:12:53.340
some of the most, with
the bloodiest records,

00:12:53.340 --> 00:12:54.750
were released from the force

00:12:54.750 --> 00:12:56.820
just before the investigation began.

00:12:56.820 --> 00:12:59.220
And so there were crimes
that were documented

00:12:59.220 --> 00:13:01.490
that were never heard
before the committee.

00:13:01.490 --> 00:13:03.610
But in many cases like the Texas Rangers

00:13:03.610 --> 00:13:06.230
that participated in
the Porvenir massacre,

00:13:06.230 --> 00:13:07.670
some of them that were released

00:13:07.670 --> 00:13:12.270
went on to become sheriffs to
work in local law enforcement,

00:13:12.270 --> 00:13:14.090
and some of them actually returned

00:13:14.090 --> 00:13:16.470
back to the Texas Ranger
force years later.

00:13:16.470 --> 00:13:20.460
And so I've tried to, in this project

00:13:20.460 --> 00:13:25.460
expose the longer roots of
this period of violence,

00:13:26.257 --> 00:13:28.590
and how it shaped institutions of policing

00:13:28.590 --> 00:13:29.870
and cultures of policing,

00:13:29.870 --> 00:13:34.180
and to raise concerns
about the periodization

00:13:34.180 --> 00:13:38.283
of this period of violence ending in 1920.

00:13:39.620 --> 00:13:43.760
And that's, you know, one of
the other parts of this project

00:13:43.760 --> 00:13:47.690
was to also show the ways
in which policing culture

00:13:47.690 --> 00:13:51.700
shapes societies more broadly.

00:13:51.700 --> 00:13:55.080
And so in understandings of
racial violence in US history,

00:13:55.080 --> 00:13:58.970
most often people think
about mob violence,

00:13:58.970 --> 00:14:01.330
so think about lynchings.

00:14:01.330 --> 00:14:03.490
And in the history in Texas,

00:14:03.490 --> 00:14:05.900
there's not only a deep
history of lynching,

00:14:05.900 --> 00:14:08.830
so the NAACP ranked
Texas third in the nation

00:14:08.830 --> 00:14:10.810
for having the highest
numbers of lynchings,

00:14:10.810 --> 00:14:13.360
just behind Georgia and Mississippi,

00:14:13.360 --> 00:14:16.260
for the numbers of anti-black lynchings.

00:14:16.260 --> 00:14:20.290
In Texas between 1848 and 1928,

00:14:20.290 --> 00:14:25.170
Texans lynched over 200 ethnic Mexicans.

00:14:25.170 --> 00:14:27.360
And so there is a deep
culture of mob violence,

00:14:27.360 --> 00:14:28.520
but some of the work that I do

00:14:28.520 --> 00:14:30.540
is to actually show the ways

00:14:30.540 --> 00:14:35.010
in which the police themselves
were agents of racial terror,

00:14:35.010 --> 00:14:37.670
practicing extralegal forms of violence,

00:14:37.670 --> 00:14:40.610
and targeting multiple
racial and ethnic groups.

00:14:40.610 --> 00:14:42.410
And so one of the the projects

00:14:42.410 --> 00:14:45.090
that I developed after I left Yale

00:14:45.090 --> 00:14:48.420
was to study the role of the Texas Rangers

00:14:48.420 --> 00:14:52.310
in intimidating NAACP
chapters across the state,

00:14:52.310 --> 00:14:54.810
and participating in mob violence

00:14:54.810 --> 00:14:56.710
by leaving prisoners unarmed.

00:14:56.710 --> 00:14:59.330
And so part of the work
too is trying to expose

00:14:59.330 --> 00:15:01.180
how these histories of racial violence

00:15:01.180 --> 00:15:03.000
are deeply interconnected.

00:15:03.000 --> 00:15:05.180
In a place like Texas so you can map

00:15:05.180 --> 00:15:10.010
how the state is acting to
create this reign of terror.

00:15:10.010 --> 00:15:13.130
But for too long, violence
at the hands of police

00:15:13.130 --> 00:15:16.520
has been shielded in a
cloak of legal authority.

00:15:16.520 --> 00:15:19.769
And in large part that's
because of the reliance

00:15:19.769 --> 00:15:23.340
of early historians like
Walter Prescott Webb

00:15:23.340 --> 00:15:26.890
on the use of Texas Ranger records,

00:15:26.890 --> 00:15:30.440
and seeing what Rangers
were producing as truth.

00:15:30.440 --> 00:15:33.320
And so I wanna read,

00:15:33.320 --> 00:15:37.050
so I'll do a little, a
reading from the book

00:15:37.050 --> 00:15:39.750
before moving on to two other points

00:15:39.750 --> 00:15:42.410
about the the interventions of the book.

00:15:42.410 --> 00:15:47.410
So I open with, for people
who are not from Texas,

00:15:47.430 --> 00:15:48.790
a scene from Brownsville

00:15:48.790 --> 00:15:51.900
at the southernmost point of the state.

00:15:51.900 --> 00:15:55.110
Brownsville borders Matamoros,
you can see it there.

00:15:55.110 --> 00:15:58.180
And here's a picture from
Robert Runyon's collection,

00:15:58.180 --> 00:16:01.140
an aerial photograph
of the US-Mexico border

00:16:01.140 --> 00:16:02.710
in the 19-teens.

00:16:02.710 --> 00:16:04.792
the community on the south,

00:16:04.792 --> 00:16:07.320
just south of the border is Matamoros,

00:16:07.320 --> 00:16:09.140
and north is Brownsville.

00:16:09.140 --> 00:16:10.020
And so you get the sense

00:16:10.020 --> 00:16:12.290
of how intimately connected
these communities were.

00:16:12.290 --> 00:16:14.930
People would live on one side of the river

00:16:14.930 --> 00:16:16.390
and owned a business on the other side.

00:16:16.390 --> 00:16:18.010
People moved fluidly.

00:16:18.010 --> 00:16:20.650
And so the calls to militarize
the border in this era

00:16:20.650 --> 00:16:24.170
really was trying to create
a divide between communities.

00:16:24.170 --> 00:16:27.020
And so that was something
that new Anglo settlers

00:16:27.020 --> 00:16:30.060
that arrived in South Texas
in the early 20th century

00:16:30.060 --> 00:16:33.490
wanted to create an international border

00:16:33.490 --> 00:16:37.403
that felt like a division,
rather than just a river.

00:16:38.890 --> 00:16:43.153
So I open the book with the
story of Florencio Garcia.

00:16:44.660 --> 00:16:48.760
It was an otherwise ordinary
day in early April 1918,

00:16:48.760 --> 00:16:51.880
but Miguel Garcia, a Mexican
national living in Texas,

00:16:51.880 --> 00:16:53.650
was growing concerned.

00:16:53.650 --> 00:16:55.970
His son Florencio, 25 years old,

00:16:55.970 --> 00:16:57.140
had not yet returned home

00:16:57.140 --> 00:17:00.240
from his job as a cattle
herder in Cameron County.

00:17:00.240 --> 00:17:01.980
Garcia started walking through town

00:17:01.980 --> 00:17:03.840
asking relatives, friends, neighbors,

00:17:03.840 --> 00:17:06.230
and eventually even the county attorney

00:17:06.230 --> 00:17:08.040
and local law enforcement officers

00:17:08.040 --> 00:17:10.310
if they had seen Florencio.

00:17:10.310 --> 00:17:12.770
Days passed without news.

00:17:12.770 --> 00:17:14.950
Then someone told Garcia
that Texas Rangers

00:17:14.950 --> 00:17:17.820
had arrested Florencio
over a week earlier,

00:17:17.820 --> 00:17:20.923
on April 5th, just south of Brownsville.

00:17:21.960 --> 00:17:25.060
Garcia knew that when
ethnic Mexicans disappeared

00:17:25.060 --> 00:17:26.310
after being arrested,

00:17:26.310 --> 00:17:28.360
the prisoners remains were often found

00:17:28.360 --> 00:17:30.470
hidden in the grove of mesquite trees

00:17:30.470 --> 00:17:32.950
in the rural Texas landscape.

00:17:32.950 --> 00:17:34.760
This grim news prompted Garcia

00:17:34.760 --> 00:17:37.290
to start searching the
brush in the countryside

00:17:37.290 --> 00:17:38.883
for his son's remains.

00:17:40.100 --> 00:17:42.150
Miguel Garcia repeatedly asked for help

00:17:42.150 --> 00:17:43.780
from local authorities.

00:17:43.780 --> 00:17:46.820
Oscar Dancy, the Cameron County Attorney,

00:17:46.820 --> 00:17:49.620
remembered Garcia's persistence.

00:17:49.620 --> 00:17:54.190
The old man, the father of
the boy, was at my office.

00:17:54.190 --> 00:17:57.080
He was at my office two or three times,

00:17:57.080 --> 00:17:58.603
and my residence once.

00:17:59.850 --> 00:18:02.450
Weeks later, local residents
found human remains

00:18:02.450 --> 00:18:07.090
outside of Brownsville, Dancy remembered.

00:18:07.090 --> 00:18:09.530
We found the bones of a human being.

00:18:09.530 --> 00:18:11.430
We found a pair of pants and a jumper,

00:18:11.430 --> 00:18:15.500
and as I recollect it, a
shirt, black hair, and a hat.

00:18:15.500 --> 00:18:18.620
A light Texas cowboy Stetson hat.

00:18:18.620 --> 00:18:19.700
And he added that there were

00:18:19.700 --> 00:18:23.373
bullet holes noticed in the
jacket, two perhaps three.

00:18:24.940 --> 00:18:26.620
The clothing matched the description

00:18:26.620 --> 00:18:28.650
Miguel Garcia had previously provided,

00:18:28.650 --> 00:18:30.319
and the investigation suggested

00:18:30.319 --> 00:18:33.460
that the body had been shot
in the back three times.

00:18:33.460 --> 00:18:35.830
The remains consisted only of clothes,

00:18:35.830 --> 00:18:37.760
a skull with a tuft of hair,

00:18:37.760 --> 00:18:41.480
and bones scattered as far as 300 yards,

00:18:41.480 --> 00:18:44.360
likely by coyotes or buzzards.

00:18:44.360 --> 00:18:46.000
Garcia arrived at the scene,

00:18:46.000 --> 00:18:49.120
and identified the
shoes, hat, and clothing

00:18:49.120 --> 00:18:50.513
as belonging to Florencio.

00:18:51.620 --> 00:18:53.700
The father pulled a
monographed handkerchief

00:18:53.700 --> 00:18:56.630
out of the jacket they had discovered,

00:18:56.630 --> 00:18:59.133
and verified that it was his son's.

00:19:00.890 --> 00:19:05.650
So the story of Miguel Garcia
looking for his father,

00:19:05.650 --> 00:19:07.990
I decided to write about this

00:19:07.990 --> 00:19:12.140
to open the book for a few reasons.

00:19:12.140 --> 00:19:13.470
What ended up playing out,

00:19:13.470 --> 00:19:15.640
what I ended up finding in the archives,

00:19:15.640 --> 00:19:19.110
thanks to the investigations
by the Mexican Consulate

00:19:19.110 --> 00:19:21.950
that investigated this case,

00:19:21.950 --> 00:19:25.460
was that there were witnesses

00:19:25.460 --> 00:19:29.570
to Florencio being in the
custody of the state police.

00:19:29.570 --> 00:19:31.480
There were witnesses that found him,

00:19:31.480 --> 00:19:35.573
that saw him being held
in a jail, county jail.

00:19:37.150 --> 00:19:38.410
And then there were witnesses

00:19:38.410 --> 00:19:40.320
that saw him leaving the county jail

00:19:40.320 --> 00:19:42.840
in the custody of Texas Rangers.

00:19:42.840 --> 00:19:46.060
And so from this account,

00:19:46.060 --> 00:19:49.760
Miguel Garcia pressured local authorities

00:19:49.760 --> 00:19:52.900
to investigate the murder of his son.

00:19:52.900 --> 00:19:54.880
And he also pressured local authorities

00:19:54.880 --> 00:19:58.660
to offer to write a death
certificate for his son.

00:19:58.660 --> 00:20:02.530
And so this became crucially
important in the archives

00:20:02.530 --> 00:20:04.720
for me being able to tell the history,

00:20:04.720 --> 00:20:07.790
not only of the father's
efforts, of the family's efforts,

00:20:07.790 --> 00:20:10.600
but to find a document that confirmed

00:20:10.600 --> 00:20:12.260
that Florencio Garcia died.

00:20:12.260 --> 00:20:14.400
And so this is an important find.

00:20:14.400 --> 00:20:15.880
You know, it includes information,

00:20:15.880 --> 00:20:18.233
biographical information about Florencio,

00:20:19.080 --> 00:20:20.810
his date of birth, for example,

00:20:20.810 --> 00:20:22.960
his occupation as a cattle herder,

00:20:22.960 --> 00:20:25.410
the birthplace, his birthplace,

00:20:25.410 --> 00:20:27.540
but also the names of his parents.

00:20:27.540 --> 00:20:31.610
We don't have here access
to the name of his wife,

00:20:31.610 --> 00:20:32.940
but we have information

00:20:32.940 --> 00:20:36.200
about who testified and
identified the body.

00:20:36.200 --> 00:20:37.740
It was Miguel Garcia.

00:20:37.740 --> 00:20:41.720
We also have information here
about the date of his burial.

00:20:41.720 --> 00:20:43.120
So we know that his family

00:20:43.120 --> 00:20:45.600
did bury the remains of Florencio.

00:20:45.600 --> 00:20:48.663
But we also see an interesting note here

00:20:51.570 --> 00:20:54.930
where the coroner actually
had to amend the document.

00:20:54.930 --> 00:20:57.340
And so he had to amend the primary source,

00:20:57.340 --> 00:21:00.050
because of the conditions
under which the body was found.

00:21:00.050 --> 00:21:03.610
And so he didn't have
information about when he died,

00:21:03.610 --> 00:21:05.080
he didn't have information, you know,

00:21:05.080 --> 00:21:07.880
he couldn't say the last
I saw him or her alive

00:21:07.880 --> 00:21:08.830
was on this date.

00:21:08.830 --> 00:21:13.160
And so he actually scratched
through the text on the form,

00:21:13.160 --> 00:21:14.770
and said that I attended,

00:21:14.770 --> 00:21:17.760
that I found the identified remains,

00:21:17.760 --> 00:21:20.320
and the death occurred on unknown date.

00:21:20.320 --> 00:21:23.660
And so with this interesting
moment in the archives

00:21:23.660 --> 00:21:26.620
where you see that this body
that the remains of Florencio

00:21:26.620 --> 00:21:28.670
is literally falling out of the ways

00:21:28.670 --> 00:21:30.600
in which a coroner would traditionally

00:21:30.600 --> 00:21:32.410
write one of these documents.

00:21:32.410 --> 00:21:35.070
And of course here, it
must have been unsettling

00:21:35.070 --> 00:21:38.060
for the family to see that the
cause of death was unknown,

00:21:38.060 --> 00:21:40.200
especially given that they found,

00:21:40.200 --> 00:21:41.360
and the investigation found,

00:21:41.360 --> 00:21:43.260
that there were bullet holes
in the back of the jacket.

00:21:43.260 --> 00:21:47.030
But because the remains
had been so far decomposed,

00:21:47.030 --> 00:21:49.230
the coroner, I'm guessing,

00:21:49.230 --> 00:21:52.030
is one of the reasons that they use to not

00:21:53.500 --> 00:21:55.550
include that that the cause of death

00:21:55.550 --> 00:21:59.220
was being shot or murdered.

00:21:59.220 --> 00:22:01.810
But one of the reasons why this document

00:22:01.810 --> 00:22:04.270
is also so important

00:22:04.270 --> 00:22:08.850
is that it directly
contrasts and undermines

00:22:08.850 --> 00:22:10.670
the efforts by the Texas Rangers

00:22:10.670 --> 00:22:12.350
that participated in arresting Florencio,

00:22:12.350 --> 00:22:15.870
and that had been identified
as having him in his custody

00:22:15.870 --> 00:22:18.010
when he was last seen alive.

00:22:18.010 --> 00:22:20.940
They actually wrote and testified

00:22:20.940 --> 00:22:23.550
that they had released Florencio,

00:22:23.550 --> 00:22:24.930
that they caused him no harm,

00:22:24.930 --> 00:22:27.080
and that actually that he had been seen

00:22:27.080 --> 00:22:29.400
across the border in Matamoros.

00:22:29.400 --> 00:22:31.560
And that perhaps he just didn't want,

00:22:31.560 --> 00:22:33.925
he wanted to abandon his family.

00:22:33.925 --> 00:22:38.580
And so despite this death
certificate being written,

00:22:38.580 --> 00:22:42.760
despite Florencio being
buried by his family,

00:22:42.760 --> 00:22:43.871
there's not only,

00:22:43.871 --> 00:22:47.130
the Texas Rangers continued to deny

00:22:47.130 --> 00:22:49.500
any involvement in his death.

00:22:49.500 --> 00:22:51.870
But with this death certificate in hand,

00:22:51.870 --> 00:22:55.230
Florencio Garcia's family was able to have

00:22:55.230 --> 00:22:57.660
the three Texas Rangers arrested.

00:22:57.660 --> 00:23:00.640
They were released on bond
and placed back into custody

00:23:00.640 --> 00:23:03.430
of their, the supervision of the captain

00:23:03.430 --> 00:23:05.710
that they worked under,

00:23:05.710 --> 00:23:10.510
and a grand jury decided not
to indict the Texas Rangers.

00:23:10.510 --> 00:23:14.000
And so there's no prosecution
of the Texas Rangers,

00:23:14.000 --> 00:23:16.330
but we do have this death certificate,

00:23:16.330 --> 00:23:18.320
and for the estimates

00:23:18.320 --> 00:23:21.100
that hundreds of ethnic
Mexicans died in this period

00:23:21.100 --> 00:23:24.780
is an estimate because part
of the acts of violence

00:23:24.780 --> 00:23:27.790
was that there was no consistent effort

00:23:27.790 --> 00:23:29.220
to record the deaths.

00:23:29.220 --> 00:23:32.790
And so we have access to
the writings of journalists

00:23:32.790 --> 00:23:35.490
who wrote articles about these deaths,

00:23:35.490 --> 00:23:38.240
we have access to some
of these rare documents,

00:23:38.240 --> 00:23:39.840
like these death certificates,

00:23:39.840 --> 00:23:42.000
Access to Mexican consulate records,

00:23:42.000 --> 00:23:45.270
so in this case because
Florencio was a Mexican national,

00:23:45.270 --> 00:23:47.470
the Mexican consul could be involved

00:23:47.470 --> 00:23:49.397
in calling for an investigation,

00:23:49.397 --> 00:23:51.600
and in large part, that's what led to

00:23:51.600 --> 00:23:53.580
at least the arrest of the Texas Rangers,

00:23:53.580 --> 00:23:57.670
and the pressure to have this
death certificate issued.

00:23:57.670 --> 00:24:02.400
But of course, the grand jury was not

00:24:02.400 --> 00:24:04.310
under the control of the Mexican consul

00:24:04.310 --> 00:24:05.930
or the requests of the father,

00:24:05.930 --> 00:24:10.030
and so there's no prosecution
of the Texas Rangers here.

00:24:10.030 --> 00:24:15.030
And the the effort to undermine

00:24:15.110 --> 00:24:17.740
the pleas and the calls for justice

00:24:17.740 --> 00:24:19.860
that we see not only in the effort,

00:24:19.860 --> 00:24:22.050
the several attempts of Miguel Garcia

00:24:22.050 --> 00:24:23.650
to go and ask the county attorney

00:24:23.650 --> 00:24:25.450
for help in finding his son,

00:24:25.450 --> 00:24:28.530
and then asking for the death
certificates to be issued,

00:24:28.530 --> 00:24:31.240
then calling for the arrest of his son,

00:24:31.240 --> 00:24:34.920
we see that all disavowed by the state,

00:24:34.920 --> 00:24:38.040
by the Texas Ranger Captain Stevens,

00:24:38.040 --> 00:24:42.050
who testified in the Gonzalez
investigation in 1919.

00:24:42.050 --> 00:24:47.050
And he in his report disputed

00:24:47.440 --> 00:24:49.890
that Florencio Garcia even died.

00:24:49.890 --> 00:24:51.780
So here we see in the questions,

00:24:51.780 --> 00:24:54.390
did you interrogate the men
and investigate the facts?

00:24:54.390 --> 00:24:55.900
And did you decide in your own mind

00:24:55.900 --> 00:24:57.840
whether or not they had
anything to do with the death?

00:24:57.840 --> 00:24:59.100
If he is dead?

00:24:59.100 --> 00:25:01.080
He's being interviewed
here by the attorneys

00:25:01.080 --> 00:25:02.900
representing the state.

00:25:02.900 --> 00:25:04.070
And Stevens replies,

00:25:04.070 --> 00:25:05.660
I investigated the statements they made,

00:25:05.660 --> 00:25:06.700
and I went to Brownsville,

00:25:06.700 --> 00:25:07.980
and I heard through a Mexican

00:25:07.980 --> 00:25:10.400
that this man was seen across the river.

00:25:10.400 --> 00:25:11.710
After they turned him loose?

00:25:11.710 --> 00:25:13.210
Yes, after they turned him loose.

00:25:13.210 --> 00:25:15.430
I spoke to deputy sheriff
who made the investigation,

00:25:15.430 --> 00:25:17.490
and he stated that he did not think

00:25:17.490 --> 00:25:19.220
that this was the body of Garcia,

00:25:19.220 --> 00:25:22.160
that those bones had been
there quite a long time.

00:25:22.160 --> 00:25:24.170
Right so again, they're also disputing

00:25:24.170 --> 00:25:27.140
the identification of the son's remains.

00:25:27.140 --> 00:25:28.900
You don't know that Florencio Garcia

00:25:30.380 --> 00:25:32.530
had a reputation of being
a notorious cow thief

00:25:32.530 --> 00:25:34.430
on the other side of the river.

00:25:34.430 --> 00:25:37.080
I heard this after the investigation.

00:25:37.080 --> 00:25:39.970
And that there was cooperation
with the authorities.

00:25:39.970 --> 00:25:42.960
And so the the investigation goes on.

00:25:42.960 --> 00:25:46.626
But in this document, we
see that Florencio Garcia,

00:25:46.626 --> 00:25:48.870
they're still denying that he died,

00:25:48.870 --> 00:25:51.170
they're still denying the
identification of him,

00:25:51.170 --> 00:25:54.930
and you also see the
criminalization of the dead, right.

00:25:54.930 --> 00:25:57.590
And do you know, and
you don't know, right,

00:25:57.590 --> 00:26:00.760
that he was a notorious cow thief.

00:26:00.760 --> 00:26:04.210
So this pattern in 1918

00:26:06.040 --> 00:26:09.210
gives us just a glimpse
into the other cases.

00:26:09.210 --> 00:26:11.330
In this case, we actually
have a name of the victim,

00:26:11.330 --> 00:26:14.150
but there's many others that
we don't have the names of.

00:26:14.150 --> 00:26:17.010
And so this was, this case and others

00:26:17.010 --> 00:26:19.200
are important for also putting on display

00:26:19.200 --> 00:26:22.560
and exposing the ways in
which the state authorities

00:26:22.560 --> 00:26:24.730
participated in justifying violence,

00:26:24.730 --> 00:26:28.230
covering crimes, and
criminalizing the dead.

00:26:28.230 --> 00:26:30.420
And that is something that in,

00:26:30.420 --> 00:26:33.440
and even in the public mainstream accounts

00:26:33.440 --> 00:26:35.300
of this period that celebrated violence

00:26:35.300 --> 00:26:38.510
or that justified or
mitigated the violence,

00:26:38.510 --> 00:26:42.380
they often cast blame on rogue agents.

00:26:42.380 --> 00:26:44.280
And so the work that I did in the book

00:26:44.280 --> 00:26:48.270
is also to expose the roles
of governors, US Congressmen,

00:26:48.270 --> 00:26:51.030
that called for violence and said things,

00:26:51.030 --> 00:26:52.747
like Congressman Hudspeth said,

00:26:52.747 --> 00:26:56.327
"I will lead a mob against
them, those bandits.

00:26:56.327 --> 00:26:58.800
"You've got to shoot
them when you see them."

00:26:58.800 --> 00:27:00.820
Right, so this is also the namesake

00:27:00.820 --> 00:27:02.980
of Hudspeth County in West Texas,

00:27:02.980 --> 00:27:06.210
so who also then went on in 1924

00:27:06.210 --> 00:27:10.130
to write into legislation
a one million dollar rider

00:27:10.130 --> 00:27:15.080
to the Immigration Act of 1924
to build the Border Patrol.

00:27:15.080 --> 00:27:18.020
So Kelley Lydell Adnandis
has done excellent work

00:27:18.020 --> 00:27:20.930
in tracing the roots of the
development of the Border Patrol

00:27:20.930 --> 00:27:25.470
being built by former Texas Rangers.

00:27:25.470 --> 00:27:29.200
But men like Hudspeth,
who called for violence

00:27:29.200 --> 00:27:32.280
and justified even mob violence,

00:27:32.280 --> 00:27:37.280
imagined a federal force built-in
like of the Texas Rangers.

00:27:38.090 --> 00:27:40.793
So enough about the state.

00:27:42.580 --> 00:27:44.700
The other sort of key departure in my book

00:27:44.700 --> 00:27:49.030
is really utilizing
feminist research methods.

00:27:49.030 --> 00:27:54.030
And so in the period of the
examinations of this violence,

00:27:54.710 --> 00:27:56.960
the work before had primarily focused

00:27:56.960 --> 00:28:00.650
on conflict between men,
Anglo men and Mexican men.

00:28:00.650 --> 00:28:02.410
And the work that I did,

00:28:02.410 --> 00:28:05.270
being really inspired by my training

00:28:05.270 --> 00:28:07.370
in gender and sexuality studies

00:28:08.280 --> 00:28:09.360
and women and gender studies,

00:28:09.360 --> 00:28:14.000
But also in critical analysis

00:28:14.000 --> 00:28:16.840
of the work of women and mothers

00:28:16.840 --> 00:28:19.700
in calling out injustice in Latin America

00:28:21.020 --> 00:28:22.620
throughout the 20th century,

00:28:22.620 --> 00:28:27.620
and most recently in the 1980s
and 90s and through today.

00:28:27.860 --> 00:28:30.910
I was really captivated
by what it meant to study

00:28:31.810 --> 00:28:34.140
and document as a historian

00:28:34.140 --> 00:28:37.030
what it was like to live
in a reign of terror.

00:28:37.030 --> 00:28:41.230
And so that requires that
we examine the aftermath.

00:28:41.230 --> 00:28:44.630
And a lot of the the
good foundational work

00:28:44.630 --> 00:28:46.680
in borderlands history has contributed to

00:28:46.680 --> 00:28:48.680
collecting the names of people who died,

00:28:48.680 --> 00:28:50.830
you know, a body count.

00:28:50.830 --> 00:28:54.010
But if we think about
trying to get a glimpse

00:28:54.010 --> 00:28:57.110
into how terror shaped daily life,

00:28:57.110 --> 00:28:59.070
it requires us to ask what happened

00:28:59.070 --> 00:29:02.570
after Florencio Garcia was buried.

00:29:02.570 --> 00:29:05.660
And in that case, I don't
have any more information

00:29:05.660 --> 00:29:08.120
into where the family
moved, where they left,

00:29:08.120 --> 00:29:11.770
but other signal cases that
I selected for the book

00:29:11.770 --> 00:29:16.010
gave us access to the longer legacy

00:29:16.010 --> 00:29:17.840
that these acts of violence had

00:29:17.840 --> 00:29:20.340
in shaping generations of families,

00:29:20.340 --> 00:29:24.320
but also shaping communities
for over a century.

00:29:24.320 --> 00:29:26.470
And this is also building on the work,

00:29:26.470 --> 00:29:29.540
like other historians
like Kidada Williams,

00:29:29.540 --> 00:29:31.860
who insist that historians have to do

00:29:31.860 --> 00:29:35.293
more than just study the violence
events of racial violence.

00:29:36.780 --> 00:29:41.600
So this has helped to really send her

00:29:41.600 --> 00:29:45.900
the work of women, and also
provide a gendered understanding

00:29:45.900 --> 00:29:49.120
of these kind, the dynamics
of racial violence.

00:29:49.120 --> 00:29:52.200
And what I found was that in erasing

00:29:52.200 --> 00:29:56.660
or forgetting what happened
in the aftermath of violence

00:29:56.660 --> 00:29:59.660
we have foreclosed the
possibilities of exposing

00:29:59.660 --> 00:30:02.160
the methods that were
available for seeking justice

00:30:02.160 --> 00:30:03.490
for the surviving relatives.

00:30:03.490 --> 00:30:08.400
So finding, you know, having,
pushing for an investigation,

00:30:08.400 --> 00:30:10.410
pushing for a death certificate.

00:30:10.410 --> 00:30:14.270
But I also found the work
of widows and children

00:30:14.270 --> 00:30:19.210
who filed legislation lawsuits
against the US government.

00:30:19.210 --> 00:30:20.870
Namely Mexican nationals

00:30:20.870 --> 00:30:24.760
that sought to hold the state accountable

00:30:24.760 --> 00:30:28.030
for the murder of their
husbands or their children.

00:30:28.030 --> 00:30:31.860
And so this was really an
important legal archive

00:30:31.860 --> 00:30:34.970
to stumble into, because it gave us access

00:30:34.970 --> 00:30:37.080
to what happened for Mexican nationals,

00:30:37.080 --> 00:30:38.090
what were the possibilities

00:30:38.090 --> 00:30:41.020
when local courts fail to prosecute.

00:30:41.020 --> 00:30:42.700
And so in some cases,

00:30:42.700 --> 00:30:44.740
there were families who did receive

00:30:44.740 --> 00:30:47.220
indemnities from the US government

00:30:47.220 --> 00:30:49.800
for either the failure of the state

00:30:49.800 --> 00:30:51.830
to prosecute mob participants

00:30:51.830 --> 00:30:55.450
or for the direct
involvement of US soldiers

00:30:55.450 --> 00:30:58.360
or law enforcement agents
like the Texas Rangers

00:30:58.360 --> 00:31:00.143
in the murder of their children.

00:31:01.290 --> 00:31:03.900
But you know, I also was really attentive

00:31:03.900 --> 00:31:08.770
to the the burden that
these families had to carry

00:31:08.770 --> 00:31:12.420
in testifying repeatedly
throughout the investigations

00:31:12.420 --> 00:31:16.600
to Mexican consuls, to US
consuls, to county officials,

00:31:16.600 --> 00:31:21.020
and thinking through the dynamics

00:31:21.020 --> 00:31:24.690
of having to attend to the
violence of on the body

00:31:24.690 --> 00:31:26.540
in an effort to seek justice.

00:31:26.540 --> 00:31:30.860
And so that introduced me to

00:31:32.550 --> 00:31:37.040
not just the work of women
in in seeking justice

00:31:37.040 --> 00:31:38.270
immediately in the aftermath,

00:31:38.270 --> 00:31:40.840
but it also introduced me to families

00:31:40.840 --> 00:31:45.630
who had been connected by
this period of violence

00:31:45.630 --> 00:31:48.380
by a broadly felt injustice.

00:31:48.380 --> 00:31:50.470
And so it was an injustice,

00:31:50.470 --> 00:31:52.750
not just from learning the histories

00:31:52.750 --> 00:31:56.740
and learning about the violence
the violent acts themselves,

00:31:56.740 --> 00:31:59.540
learning that people weren't prosecuted

00:31:59.540 --> 00:32:00.720
in this period of violence,

00:32:00.720 --> 00:32:03.400
but also learning that
this period of violence

00:32:03.400 --> 00:32:05.600
had been disavowed by the state,

00:32:05.600 --> 00:32:08.570
or erased or silenced or celebrated.

00:32:08.570 --> 00:32:13.370
And so thinking about injustice
here, it's multifaceted.

00:32:13.370 --> 00:32:18.370
So it's the injustice of what
took place in the 19-teens,

00:32:18.450 --> 00:32:20.810
but then it's also the continued

00:32:20.810 --> 00:32:22.860
injustices that these families carried.

00:32:22.860 --> 00:32:27.340
And so one person described this to me as,

00:32:27.340 --> 00:32:28.981
Norma Longoria Rodriguez is

00:32:28.981 --> 00:32:31.020
one of the leading Texas residents

00:32:31.020 --> 00:32:33.640
who I interviewed about the double murder

00:32:33.640 --> 00:32:36.290
of her grandfather and great-grandfather,

00:32:36.290 --> 00:32:38.250
Jesus Bazan and Antonio Longoria.

00:32:38.250 --> 00:32:42.310
They were murdered by a
posse of a Texas Ranger

00:32:42.310 --> 00:32:47.050
and two civilians, Paul
West and William Sterling.

00:32:47.050 --> 00:32:49.010
William Sterling went on later

00:32:49.010 --> 00:32:52.150
to lead the Texas Ranger force.

00:32:52.150 --> 00:32:56.830
But in September 1915, her
grandfather and great-grandfather

00:32:56.830 --> 00:33:01.310
visited a Texas Ranger
a camp in South Texas

00:33:01.310 --> 00:33:05.710
in Hidalgo County to report
that they had been robbed.

00:33:05.710 --> 00:33:07.730
And so they wanted to report

00:33:07.730 --> 00:33:10.250
that they had had horses stolen.

00:33:10.250 --> 00:33:11.530
In the case that the people

00:33:11.530 --> 00:33:14.140
that stole these horses
were later arrested,

00:33:14.140 --> 00:33:16.740
the family didn't want these horses

00:33:16.740 --> 00:33:20.520
in the custody of these
men to then bring suspicion

00:33:20.520 --> 00:33:22.070
of them supporting banditry.

00:33:22.070 --> 00:33:24.440
And in this era even being suspected

00:33:24.440 --> 00:33:27.483
or just called being
suspected of banditry,

00:33:29.197 --> 00:33:33.500
or sympathizing with bandits,
was a license to kill.

00:33:33.500 --> 00:33:37.790
And so on the way home, Jesus
Bazan and Antonio Longoria,

00:33:37.790 --> 00:33:40.490
you know, it's reported,
witnesses later reported,

00:33:40.490 --> 00:33:42.730
that they mounted their horses

00:33:42.730 --> 00:33:46.070
to return back to their ranch,
and when they had reached

00:33:46.070 --> 00:33:49.880
about 300 yards away
from the ranger campsite,

00:33:49.880 --> 00:33:52.730
these three men loaded
into a Model T Ford,

00:33:52.730 --> 00:33:55.670
drove to the men, and shot
them both in the back.

00:33:55.670 --> 00:33:59.610
So part of the devastating
history of this account

00:33:59.610 --> 00:34:03.320
was not only the brutality
of the cold-blooded murder,

00:34:03.320 --> 00:34:07.590
but that also that Texas
Ranger Captain Harry Ransom

00:34:07.590 --> 00:34:11.010
ordered the witnesses,
the laborers on the ranch,

00:34:11.010 --> 00:34:12.440
not to bury the remains.

00:34:12.440 --> 00:34:13.920
Not to tend to the remains,

00:34:13.920 --> 00:34:15.430
but also not to bury the remains.

00:34:15.430 --> 00:34:17.350
And so this was an extreme act

00:34:17.350 --> 00:34:21.713
of disrespect and intimidation.

00:34:22.690 --> 00:34:26.160
And so a few days passed

00:34:26.160 --> 00:34:30.740
before friends of Bazan and Longoria

00:34:30.740 --> 00:34:33.483
took the risk and actually
buried the remains.

00:34:34.470 --> 00:34:37.870
But when Norma Longoria
Rodriguez learned of this story,

00:34:37.870 --> 00:34:39.760
it really interrupted her life,

00:34:39.760 --> 00:34:43.190
and it filled her with a sense of grief,

00:34:43.190 --> 00:34:45.230
and it left her with unanswered questions.

00:34:45.230 --> 00:34:47.910
So she turned to history.

00:34:47.910 --> 00:34:51.430
She visited local libraries
to read history books

00:34:51.430 --> 00:34:52.690
about this period of violence.

00:34:52.690 --> 00:34:54.320
She wanted to, when she heard

00:34:54.320 --> 00:34:55.930
that a Texas Ranger had participated,

00:34:55.930 --> 00:34:59.650
it disrupted all of her
understandings of the Rangers.

00:34:59.650 --> 00:35:02.740
You know, these were the
Lone Ranger and Chuck Norris,

00:35:02.740 --> 00:35:06.550
the heroes that she had
grown up idolizing in school.

00:35:06.550 --> 00:35:08.090
And so she went to the history books

00:35:08.090 --> 00:35:11.370
to try to learn more about
this period of violence.

00:35:11.370 --> 00:35:13.320
And she found some books,

00:35:13.320 --> 00:35:15.797
but for the most part,
she had more questions.

00:35:15.797 --> 00:35:19.260
And so she also tried to
turn to local archives,

00:35:19.260 --> 00:35:21.140
state archives, she went to Austin

00:35:21.140 --> 00:35:23.240
to look at the Texas Ranger reports,

00:35:23.240 --> 00:35:26.390
she read through microfilm
of local newspapers,

00:35:26.390 --> 00:35:29.860
trying to find some
discussion of this case,

00:35:29.860 --> 00:35:31.260
and she didn't find anything.

00:35:31.260 --> 00:35:32.620
She didn't find a death certificate

00:35:32.620 --> 00:35:34.960
for Jesus Bazan and Antonio Longoria.

00:35:34.960 --> 00:35:39.110
And so despite dedicating
weekends and vacations

00:35:39.110 --> 00:35:41.130
to searching state archives
for public records,

00:35:41.130 --> 00:35:42.640
she didn't find these records.

00:35:42.640 --> 00:35:45.810
And so instead she took on the task

00:35:45.810 --> 00:35:47.787
of documenting her family history.

00:35:47.787 --> 00:35:50.500
And so I actually found
Norma Longoria Rodriguez.

00:35:50.500 --> 00:35:54.120
Steve Pitti in the middle
of my dissertation research

00:35:54.120 --> 00:35:55.540
forwarded me an email

00:35:55.540 --> 00:35:57.460
because she had published an essay

00:35:57.460 --> 00:35:59.170
called Silence of the Heart,

00:35:59.170 --> 00:36:02.420
which gave a history of her grandmother's,

00:36:02.420 --> 00:36:03.410
and her great-grandmother,

00:36:03.410 --> 00:36:07.480
Epigmenia Bazan and Antonia Longoria,

00:36:07.480 --> 00:36:09.750
that had to live in the wake of violence.

00:36:09.750 --> 00:36:13.200
And so she wrote a history

00:36:13.200 --> 00:36:15.800
of her grandmother and great-grandmother,

00:36:15.800 --> 00:36:17.810
and gave an account of the double murder,

00:36:17.810 --> 00:36:19.940
and she published it in a local,

00:36:19.940 --> 00:36:23.733
in a digital publication
called Somos Primos.

00:36:24.880 --> 00:36:26.594
And so I visited her,

00:36:26.594 --> 00:36:30.920
I conducted an oral history
with her for about two hours,

00:36:30.920 --> 00:36:32.760
and then she brought out a piece of,

00:36:32.760 --> 00:36:34.000
you know I thought the interview was done,

00:36:34.000 --> 00:36:35.080
so I was packing up my stuff,

00:36:35.080 --> 00:36:38.230
and then she brings out a
piece of pie and some iced tea,

00:36:38.230 --> 00:36:40.690
and then she brings out photographs,

00:36:40.690 --> 00:36:43.980
and then she brings out pictures,
oh that's the same thing.

00:36:43.980 --> 00:36:48.600
She brings put photographs
(laughs) and maps,

00:36:48.600 --> 00:36:52.880
and a teaching certificate
of Antonio Longoria's,

00:36:52.880 --> 00:36:54.720
and a letter that describes him

00:36:54.720 --> 00:36:56.410
as the Hidalgo County Commissioner.

00:36:56.410 --> 00:36:59.730
And so she started to bring me documents

00:36:59.730 --> 00:37:02.363
that proved that the two men had lived.

00:37:03.820 --> 00:37:06.960
And she showed me photographs
of the gravestones.

00:37:06.960 --> 00:37:08.450
And then she brought out documents

00:37:08.450 --> 00:37:10.470
that gave me access to who her grandmother

00:37:10.470 --> 00:37:11.710
and great-grandmother were.

00:37:11.710 --> 00:37:14.340
And she told me stories
about Antonia Longoria,

00:37:14.340 --> 00:37:16.510
who she referred to as
(speaks foreign language),

00:37:16.510 --> 00:37:18.190
you know, her grandmother.

00:37:18.190 --> 00:37:20.150
Told me about the baked
goods that she made,

00:37:20.150 --> 00:37:22.580
told me about how they leased out land,

00:37:22.580 --> 00:37:23.650
and just this weekend

00:37:23.650 --> 00:37:25.470
I learned about a grove of peach trees

00:37:25.470 --> 00:37:26.500
that they had on the ranch

00:37:26.500 --> 00:37:30.350
that died after the double murder,

00:37:30.350 --> 00:37:32.930
because the family couldn't sustain

00:37:32.930 --> 00:37:35.440
all of the work on the ranch.

00:37:35.440 --> 00:37:38.970
But when I asked her about this work

00:37:38.970 --> 00:37:41.310
and a sentiment of loss that had remained,

00:37:41.310 --> 00:37:43.790
she explained it's an injustice,

00:37:43.790 --> 00:37:47.990
it never leaves you, it's inherited loss.

00:37:47.990 --> 00:37:50.070
And the title of the book,

00:37:50.070 --> 00:37:51.660
The Injustice Never Leaves You,

00:37:51.660 --> 00:37:54.350
comes from that interview,

00:37:54.350 --> 00:37:57.200
and it's inspired by the questions

00:37:57.200 --> 00:38:01.010
that Norma Longoria
Rodriguez prompted me to ask

00:38:01.010 --> 00:38:04.190
that other historians had
not prompted me to ask.

00:38:04.190 --> 00:38:08.120
Historians are objective, stay removed,

00:38:08.120 --> 00:38:12.370
this is not the field of emotions.

00:38:12.370 --> 00:38:14.020
And one of the unanswered questions

00:38:14.020 --> 00:38:15.560
that has stayed with me

00:38:16.460 --> 00:38:20.790
is that she wished that
she had had the opportunity

00:38:20.790 --> 00:38:23.890
to ask one of the witnesses
before they passed

00:38:23.890 --> 00:38:28.323
if her grandfather and
great-grandfather died quickly.

00:38:29.270 --> 00:38:31.423
Did they have a fast death?

00:38:32.330 --> 00:38:34.310
Or did they suffer?

00:38:34.310 --> 00:38:38.030
You know, and that is
such a human question.

00:38:38.030 --> 00:38:39.980
You know, she connected intimately

00:38:39.980 --> 00:38:41.670
with this grandfather
and great-grandfather

00:38:41.670 --> 00:38:43.010
who she never knew,

00:38:43.010 --> 00:38:44.890
and more than just wanting to know

00:38:44.890 --> 00:38:46.090
the who, what, where, when, and why,

00:38:46.090 --> 00:38:47.990
what were the motives, who
were the Texas Rangers,

00:38:47.990 --> 00:38:49.180
when did it happen,

00:38:49.180 --> 00:38:51.700
she was really lingering
in those last moments,

00:38:51.700 --> 00:38:54.790
when Captain Ransom ordered people

00:38:54.790 --> 00:38:57.933
not to tend to the
bodies, were they alive?

00:38:58.950 --> 00:38:59.783
You know?

00:39:01.290 --> 00:39:02.980
Those are the questions that she has,

00:39:02.980 --> 00:39:06.400
and those are the questions
that she's continued to ask,

00:39:06.400 --> 00:39:11.290
but also, she's been committed
to making the history public.

00:39:11.290 --> 00:39:15.980
And I remember sitting in Joanne
Meyerowitz's office in HGS,

00:39:15.980 --> 00:39:18.750
and talking about Norma
Longoria Rodriguez,

00:39:18.750 --> 00:39:20.220
and the Alvarados that I had met,

00:39:20.220 --> 00:39:23.550
and saying these, they are
a part of this history.

00:39:23.550 --> 00:39:27.653
The history did not end in 1918
when the massacre happened.

00:39:28.794 --> 00:39:31.500
And they were making history,

00:39:31.500 --> 00:39:33.400
they were writing their own history.

00:39:33.400 --> 00:39:37.120
And so this is coming from the archive

00:39:37.120 --> 00:39:38.440
of Norma Longoria Rodriguez.

00:39:38.440 --> 00:39:40.980
This is a photograph of his Jesus Bazan.

00:39:40.980 --> 00:39:43.290
This was on display in the Bullock Museum.

00:39:43.290 --> 00:39:45.790
The Bullock Museum pulls from archives

00:39:45.790 --> 00:39:50.010
like the Library of Congress,
the Autry Museum in L.A.,

00:39:50.010 --> 00:39:51.160
the Texas State Archives,

00:39:51.160 --> 00:39:53.840
but they didn't know Jesus Bazan,

00:39:53.840 --> 00:39:55.763
and they didn't have a picture of him.

00:39:57.640 --> 00:40:00.350
And they also didn't have
this stunning photograph

00:40:00.350 --> 00:40:05.350
of Epigmenia Bazan, here she
is, and her granddaughters.

00:40:05.780 --> 00:40:08.630
This is Eloisa, you know,
surrounded by the plants

00:40:08.630 --> 00:40:09.550
and with the bird in the corner.

00:40:09.550 --> 00:40:11.260
I just love this photograph,

00:40:11.260 --> 00:40:13.060
and if I could be this,

00:40:13.060 --> 00:40:15.383
if I could just pose
like that all the time.

00:40:16.300 --> 00:40:20.367
But this was a photograph
that Christine Molis,

00:40:22.640 --> 00:40:25.140
her mother Melba Coody had in their home

00:40:25.140 --> 00:40:28.190
that they donated or lent
to the Bullock Museum

00:40:28.190 --> 00:40:29.510
to have on display.

00:40:29.510 --> 00:40:32.300
And they also had this photograph

00:40:32.300 --> 00:40:35.050
of Antonio Longoria and Antonia Longoria,

00:40:35.050 --> 00:40:36.860
and the inscription on the
back is really beautiful.

00:40:36.860 --> 00:40:38.420
It's written to their friends,

00:40:38.420 --> 00:40:41.123
who they call their
brothers, right, you know,

00:40:42.560 --> 00:40:43.467
the familial connection here,

00:40:43.467 --> 00:40:45.650
and so this was taken in 1895.

00:40:45.650 --> 00:40:48.403
And so these are the kinds of documents

00:40:48.403 --> 00:40:50.280
that the families had preserved,

00:40:50.280 --> 00:40:52.940
they had participated in
preserving an archive,

00:40:52.940 --> 00:40:55.780
and pulling together a
collection of documents.

00:40:55.780 --> 00:40:58.570
But she also participated
in writing history.

00:40:58.570 --> 00:41:01.120
The vernacular history making

00:41:01.120 --> 00:41:03.020
is the concept that I pulled from.

00:41:03.020 --> 00:41:05.190
It was Joanne, she said it's
vernacular history making,

00:41:05.190 --> 00:41:07.483
so there it is, it's in the book.

00:41:08.860 --> 00:41:11.160
So she published a piece most recently

00:41:11.160 --> 00:41:14.770
in La Voz de Esperanza, it comes
out of the Esperanza Center

00:41:14.770 --> 00:41:16.150
in San Antonio, Texas.

00:41:16.150 --> 00:41:21.150
And so she wrote a lyrical
(speaks foreign language)

00:41:21.210 --> 00:41:26.210
to her cousin, and his
name is Ricardo Martinez,

00:41:26.460 --> 00:41:31.460
and he helped to restore the homestead

00:41:31.700 --> 00:41:35.290
of Antonio Longoria and Antonia Longoria,

00:41:35.290 --> 00:41:40.130
and so the oral histories
that Norma Longoria Rodriguez

00:41:40.130 --> 00:41:43.340
helped to record of the
accounts of the children

00:41:43.340 --> 00:41:44.440
that were in the house

00:41:44.440 --> 00:41:48.300
the day that these men
came to take horses.

00:41:48.300 --> 00:41:49.540
He's preserved for example

00:41:49.540 --> 00:41:51.820
like the same window
that they looked out of

00:41:51.820 --> 00:41:53.667
and described in their oral histories

00:41:53.667 --> 00:41:56.610
is still in the home that he's preserved.

00:41:56.610 --> 00:41:59.610
So this is the kind of
vernacular history making

00:41:59.610 --> 00:42:01.550
that I write about in the book

00:42:01.550 --> 00:42:03.240
as being an important political,

00:42:03.240 --> 00:42:05.700
an important historical contribution,

00:42:05.700 --> 00:42:08.290
but also speaking back to the tradition

00:42:08.290 --> 00:42:09.800
that celebrates the Texas Rangers

00:42:09.800 --> 00:42:12.750
or that celebrates this
period of racial violence.

00:42:12.750 --> 00:42:15.260
But that's a tall task

00:42:15.260 --> 00:42:18.420
because we also remember
that Walter Prescott Webb

00:42:18.420 --> 00:42:21.060
wrote, published his
first canonical account

00:42:21.060 --> 00:42:23.370
of the Texas Rangers in 1935,

00:42:23.370 --> 00:42:27.030
when he described
indigenous people as savages

00:42:27.030 --> 00:42:30.830
and Mexicans as having
dirty water for blood.

00:42:30.830 --> 00:42:35.670
And that became inscribed
in in Texas history,

00:42:35.670 --> 00:42:38.590
because he helped to build
public history in Texas.

00:42:38.590 --> 00:42:41.730
And so the book coming out in 1935

00:42:41.730 --> 00:42:43.670
was influential in the celebrations

00:42:43.670 --> 00:42:45.730
of the Texas centennial in Texas.

00:42:45.730 --> 00:42:48.370
So Texans celebrate Texas independence,

00:42:48.370 --> 00:42:52.310
and so in 1936, he helped to install

00:42:52.310 --> 00:42:55.170
over 1,000 monuments
across the state of Texas,

00:42:55.170 --> 00:42:59.270
and to inspire exhibitions
at the Texas Centennial

00:42:59.270 --> 00:43:03.330
like the Junior Rangers with the pledge,

00:43:03.330 --> 00:43:05.360
like a good Ranger, I'll
be brave, honest, obedient,

00:43:05.360 --> 00:43:08.853
and always keep my eyes out for danger.

00:43:09.900 --> 00:43:11.880
And so this is actually
a pledge that students,

00:43:11.880 --> 00:43:14.740
that young generations can still take.

00:43:14.740 --> 00:43:17.190
It's sponsored by the
Texas Ranger History Museum

00:43:17.190 --> 00:43:19.350
in Waco, Texas.

00:43:19.350 --> 00:43:21.870
But of course, generations
have also been brought up

00:43:21.870 --> 00:43:24.660
watching The Lone Ranger
and Walker Texas Ranger,

00:43:24.660 --> 00:43:28.620
and so the work that
families and descendants

00:43:28.620 --> 00:43:31.210
have had to take on in
trying to commemorate

00:43:31.210 --> 00:43:33.200
these histories of racial violence,

00:43:33.200 --> 00:43:38.200
or speaking back to myths
that have been ingrained

00:43:38.990 --> 00:43:40.740
in public understandings of the past.

00:43:40.740 --> 00:43:45.740
And so I, a little bit lost for time,

00:43:46.077 --> 00:43:47.720
because I don't have a clock.

00:43:47.720 --> 00:43:48.920
What time?

00:43:48.920 --> 00:43:50.130
Okay, so here we go.

00:43:50.130 --> 00:43:52.950
So I'm gonna wrap up just by showing,

00:43:52.950 --> 00:43:55.440
okay, so then there's also
the baseball Texas Rangers,

00:43:55.440 --> 00:43:57.600
I don't know if any of
you are Rangers fans.

00:43:57.600 --> 00:44:00.500
I mean yeah, so there's
that guy, there's Bush.

00:44:00.500 --> 00:44:01.530
But then there's this.
(audience laughing)

00:44:01.530 --> 00:44:02.363
I have another one,

00:44:02.363 --> 00:44:05.028
I wish that I had one with his mother

00:44:05.028 --> 00:44:06.210
with the former first lady,

00:44:06.210 --> 00:44:07.470
because it's really charming, actually,

00:44:07.470 --> 00:44:09.860
she looks really cute in a baseball cap.

00:44:09.860 --> 00:44:13.300
But is also the Waco Texas
Ranger History Museum,

00:44:13.300 --> 00:44:17.510
and I write a review of
the museum in my book,

00:44:17.510 --> 00:44:19.900
and I was saying earlier that I thought,

00:44:19.900 --> 00:44:20.970
you know, after I was reading it,

00:44:20.970 --> 00:44:23.030
like wow that was really tough.

00:44:23.030 --> 00:44:26.770
And then I visited the museum
again, just a week ago,

00:44:26.770 --> 00:44:27.980
and I found even more,

00:44:27.980 --> 00:44:32.060
that I could have really amped
it up, actually, I think.

00:44:32.060 --> 00:44:34.440
There's a lot of work that we have to do

00:44:34.440 --> 00:44:37.930
in having a truthful
accounting of the past,

00:44:37.930 --> 00:44:40.720
encouraging cultural
institutions across the state

00:44:40.720 --> 00:44:43.460
to participate in a truthful
accounting of the past,

00:44:43.460 --> 00:44:46.280
and there are some institutions,

00:44:46.280 --> 00:44:48.520
not just this but across the state,

00:44:48.520 --> 00:44:49.750
that actually have very,

00:44:49.750 --> 00:44:52.770
that have racist depictions of the past.

00:44:52.770 --> 00:44:55.833
And I don't use that word lightly.

00:44:56.940 --> 00:45:01.940
But in working with
descendants of racial violence,

00:45:05.249 --> 00:45:07.470
and this group that I helped to start

00:45:07.470 --> 00:45:08.700
called Refusing to Forget,

00:45:08.700 --> 00:45:11.230
we started organizing in 2013

00:45:11.230 --> 00:45:13.650
to pull together public commemorations,

00:45:13.650 --> 00:45:16.610
because we recognize that
despite the dozens of books

00:45:16.610 --> 00:45:20.020
that documented this
period of racial violence,

00:45:20.020 --> 00:45:24.040
those academic, the
progress that we had made

00:45:24.040 --> 00:45:27.130
in professional conversations
and understandings of history,

00:45:27.130 --> 00:45:30.520
had not changed public
understandings of the past.

00:45:30.520 --> 00:45:34.990
And so we were thinking about
having an exhibit at UT-Austin

00:45:36.880 --> 00:45:40.320
sponsored by the Center for
Mexican-American Studies,

00:45:40.320 --> 00:45:41.760
and we were thinking of raising funds

00:45:41.760 --> 00:45:45.133
for one monument or marker,
some sort of statue,

00:45:46.020 --> 00:45:48.423
artist installation.

00:45:49.390 --> 00:45:52.210
And when we talked to
people like Norma Longoria,

00:45:52.210 --> 00:45:53.650
when we had our first organizing meeting,

00:45:53.650 --> 00:45:56.790
I invited descendants like
Norma Longoria Rodriguez

00:45:56.790 --> 00:45:57.730
and the Alvarados,

00:45:57.730 --> 00:46:00.250
who are descendants of
the Porvenir massacre,

00:46:00.250 --> 00:46:03.463
and they very adamantly said,

00:46:05.010 --> 00:46:07.220
and what is the state gonna do?

00:46:07.220 --> 00:46:09.690
So the peak of violence occurred in 2015,

00:46:09.690 --> 00:46:11.700
we had been planning for 2015,

00:46:11.700 --> 00:46:14.680
and they said, "and what
is the state gonna do?"

00:46:14.680 --> 00:46:16.500
Because we have been memorializing

00:46:16.500 --> 00:46:18.230
these histories on our own,

00:46:18.230 --> 00:46:20.200
we have been documenting them on our own,

00:46:20.200 --> 00:46:22.950
we have our own events,

00:46:22.950 --> 00:46:24.270
what is it gonna take for the state

00:46:24.270 --> 00:46:28.000
to finally acknowledge its role
in this period of violence?

00:46:28.000 --> 00:46:33.000
And so it shifted our whole,
or all of our strategies,

00:46:33.430 --> 00:46:37.020
and so we utilized the Undertold
Marker program in Texas,

00:46:37.020 --> 00:46:41.720
which is actually a phenomenal program.

00:46:41.720 --> 00:46:43.850
Since 2006, they have unveiled

00:46:43.850 --> 00:46:46.950
over 100 markers across the state,

00:46:46.950 --> 00:46:49.260
that tell undertold histories of Texas.

00:46:49.260 --> 00:46:51.610
And so what it does is
it creates an opportunity

00:46:51.610 --> 00:46:54.240
for anybody in Texas and any historian

00:46:54.240 --> 00:46:57.200
to submit an application
for an undertold marker,

00:46:57.200 --> 00:47:01.530
and it does not require
a county to nominate,

00:47:01.530 --> 00:47:03.280
to sponsor the application.

00:47:03.280 --> 00:47:06.070
And so in some of the cases
of episodes of racial violence

00:47:06.070 --> 00:47:08.460
that I have documented,

00:47:08.460 --> 00:47:11.350
the counties are opposed to
having a marker like this.

00:47:11.350 --> 00:47:14.550
And so when you submit
it directly to the state,

00:47:14.550 --> 00:47:17.090
the state can approve or
disapprove the application.

00:47:17.090 --> 00:47:20.090
I actually had to apply
for this one three times.

00:47:20.090 --> 00:47:22.670
But we just unveiled it on Saturday.

00:47:22.670 --> 00:47:24.950
And so it was tremendous.

00:47:24.950 --> 00:47:29.083
We had 175 people attended
the unveiling ceremony.

00:47:30.040 --> 00:47:32.643
We had descendants
participated, we had a blessing,

00:47:33.660 --> 00:47:35.650
and it was incredible.

00:47:35.650 --> 00:47:39.220
But we had like a three-day
public history extravaganza,

00:47:39.220 --> 00:47:42.359
and so we had a conference that day,

00:47:42.359 --> 00:47:44.720
and this is here Melba Coody,

00:47:44.720 --> 00:47:47.750
and Norma Longoria Rodriguez,
and Ricardo Martinez,

00:47:47.750 --> 00:47:51.470
and they spoke about their own
work preserving the history,

00:47:51.470 --> 00:47:52.830
how they learned the history,

00:47:52.830 --> 00:47:55.370
and then what they did
to try to make it public.

00:47:55.370 --> 00:47:58.510
and this is Christine Molis,
Melba Coody's daughter,

00:47:58.510 --> 00:47:59.670
who was really instrumental

00:47:59.670 --> 00:48:02.290
in getting the photographs
on display in the Bullock,

00:48:02.290 --> 00:48:03.123
and that's me.

00:48:03.123 --> 00:48:06.640
And then we also had a history harvest.

00:48:06.640 --> 00:48:10.140
We collaborated with
UTRGV Special Collections

00:48:10.140 --> 00:48:13.520
to give a workshop to UTRGV students

00:48:13.520 --> 00:48:17.600
on how to digitize photographs, documents,

00:48:17.600 --> 00:48:20.210
and how to conduct oral histories,

00:48:20.210 --> 00:48:21.610
and so that was my part.

00:48:21.610 --> 00:48:25.240
And we also, in terms of
the spirit of collaboration,

00:48:25.240 --> 00:48:27.830
this is Valerie Ramirez,
and she's a member

00:48:27.830 --> 00:48:30.160
of the Hidalgo County
Historical Commission.

00:48:30.160 --> 00:48:33.460
And so we had support
from local universities,

00:48:33.460 --> 00:48:35.600
from the archivists, from the students,

00:48:35.600 --> 00:48:37.750
but also from the local
County Historical Commission.

00:48:37.750 --> 00:48:39.590
And in this case, the
County Historical Commission

00:48:39.590 --> 00:48:41.810
was really supportive,

00:48:41.810 --> 00:48:45.657
but they also are not always tech savvy,

00:48:45.657 --> 00:48:48.803
so figuring out how to fill
out the application in the PDF.

00:48:49.710 --> 00:48:52.537
They also had a president of the,

00:48:52.537 --> 00:48:55.240
the Chairman who was hospitalized.

00:48:55.240 --> 00:48:57.400
And so you know this is all volunteer,

00:48:57.400 --> 00:48:59.890
and so this ended up being

00:48:59.890 --> 00:49:01.910
a tremendous opportunity
for collaboration.

00:49:01.910 --> 00:49:05.410
And so I'm hopeful about the future

00:49:05.410 --> 00:49:08.250
and possibilities for making public change

00:49:09.580 --> 00:49:11.930
through engaged public history work,

00:49:11.930 --> 00:49:16.930
but I'm also painfully aware
of the climate of this work.

00:49:17.970 --> 00:49:20.090
So most people know about Hidalgo County,

00:49:20.090 --> 00:49:22.280
or places like McAllen because of

00:49:22.280 --> 00:49:25.890
it was ground zero of family separation.

00:49:25.890 --> 00:49:29.630
In every county where we are
unveiling historical markers,

00:49:29.630 --> 00:49:33.670
there are multiple detention
centers for immigrants.

00:49:33.670 --> 00:49:36.850
You know, I wrote in
the epilogue of the book

00:49:36.850 --> 00:49:41.850
also makes direct parallels
between the families

00:49:42.400 --> 00:49:43.630
like Miguel Garcia,

00:49:43.630 --> 00:49:46.000
who were literally searching the landscape

00:49:46.000 --> 00:49:47.490
for the remains of his son,

00:49:47.490 --> 00:49:51.050
and families today who
are doing that same work.

00:49:51.050 --> 00:49:54.620
And so I'll just speed through here,

00:49:54.620 --> 00:49:58.960
but you know, (mumbles) this
is a center for human rights,

00:49:58.960 --> 00:50:03.960
and I am gripped by the roles of families

00:50:04.070 --> 00:50:07.550
who are again preserving the
names of their loved ones

00:50:07.550 --> 00:50:10.600
who disappeared when crossing
into the United States,

00:50:10.600 --> 00:50:11.890
who are calling on the state

00:50:11.890 --> 00:50:16.470
to remember the names and the
faces of their loved ones.

00:50:16.470 --> 00:50:21.160
And you know, over 6,000
people have died since 1994,

00:50:22.940 --> 00:50:25.300
and deterrence policies funneled

00:50:25.300 --> 00:50:27.390
people seeking refuge in the United States

00:50:27.390 --> 00:50:30.280
through the desert in Arizona,

00:50:30.280 --> 00:50:32.750
and through the
treacherous parts in Texas,

00:50:32.750 --> 00:50:35.410
and so literally in the
same counties in Texas

00:50:35.410 --> 00:50:37.350
where loved ones were a hundred years ago

00:50:37.350 --> 00:50:38.183
looking for the remains

00:50:38.183 --> 00:50:43.170
of their husbands and
children and daughters,

00:50:43.170 --> 00:50:44.640
families are doing the same work

00:50:44.640 --> 00:50:48.380
of trying to identify the
remains of people who have died.

00:50:48.380 --> 00:50:52.840
And so, so I'll stop there

00:50:52.840 --> 00:50:54.900
to answer any questions that people have

00:50:54.900 --> 00:50:58.170
about the public work, the history itself,

00:50:58.170 --> 00:51:03.170
the recovery work, and look
forward to a good conversation.

00:51:05.445 --> 00:51:08.695
(audience applauding)

00:51:11.829 --> 00:51:15.392
- [Woman] So Stephen had to
leave, I'm taking over for him.

00:51:15.392 --> 00:51:17.640
So would you like to field
questions, would you like me?

00:51:17.640 --> 00:51:19.020
- Sure, I can field questions.

00:51:19.020 --> 00:51:20.570
- Alright, thank you very much.

00:51:24.240 --> 00:51:25.073
- Yes?

00:51:25.073 --> 00:51:27.191
- [Sandra] Hi, my name is Sandra.

00:51:27.191 --> 00:51:28.300
I really enjoyed the book.

00:51:28.300 --> 00:51:30.606
Thank you so much for
what you're doing there

00:51:30.606 --> 00:51:34.093
and (mumbles) outside of academia.

00:51:34.093 --> 00:51:35.537
And I know you talked a
little bit about this,

00:51:35.537 --> 00:51:39.103
but I was really struck by,
when you say vernacular history,

00:51:40.050 --> 00:51:42.470
what you call it in the
book is politics of memory,

00:51:42.470 --> 00:51:45.840
and I'm just curious how you--

00:51:45.840 --> 00:51:47.190
- Oh, there's a microphone.

00:51:48.270 --> 00:51:50.040
- [Sandra] Let's try it again, sorry.

00:51:50.040 --> 00:51:53.553
- Yeah, so you were asking
about vernacular history making?

00:51:53.553 --> 00:51:54.386
- [Sandra] The politics of memory.

00:51:54.386 --> 00:51:55.219
- The politics memory.

00:51:55.219 --> 00:51:56.570
- [Sandra] Yeah, I'm really curious how,

00:51:56.570 --> 00:51:59.020
and was hoping you could reflect

00:51:59.020 --> 00:52:03.910
on your graduate training,
and getting to this approach,

00:52:03.910 --> 00:52:05.410
and understanding objectivity.

00:52:06.810 --> 00:52:09.317
And I majored in American
studies, American history,

00:52:09.317 --> 00:52:13.310
and so something I was really
enjoying about your book

00:52:13.310 --> 00:52:16.301
was how you were sort of
pushing back against that

00:52:16.301 --> 00:52:18.830
(mumbling).

00:52:18.830 --> 00:52:20.663
- Yeah, well I think you know,

00:52:21.800 --> 00:52:26.080
unfortunately there are still
a good number of historians

00:52:26.080 --> 00:52:27.860
that are skeptical of oral history.

00:52:27.860 --> 00:52:29.640
So you know, I intentionally
put that section

00:52:29.640 --> 00:52:31.770
in the book in the introduction

00:52:31.770 --> 00:52:33.520
about the possibilities of oral history.

00:52:33.520 --> 00:52:35.893
I mean, we have known the
possibilities for decades,

00:52:35.893 --> 00:52:39.480
(laughs) but I also thought that

00:52:39.480 --> 00:52:42.250
people who maybe are not
historians might read this book.

00:52:42.250 --> 00:52:45.240
And there's always a
sort of quick dismissal

00:52:45.240 --> 00:52:47.210
of oral history as evidence,

00:52:47.210 --> 00:52:49.510
and so I'm really interested

00:52:49.510 --> 00:52:54.490
not only in studying memory
and in conflicting memories,

00:52:54.490 --> 00:52:56.120
so the first chapter looks at

00:52:56.120 --> 00:52:58.480
the the lynching of Antonio Rodriguez,

00:52:58.480 --> 00:53:02.403
and really there are no
family members in that town.

00:53:03.319 --> 00:53:08.319
He is burned alive at the
stake in November in 1910,

00:53:09.060 --> 00:53:12.670
and he is understood to have
just been passing through town,

00:53:12.670 --> 00:53:14.670
or have been a migrant laborer,

00:53:14.670 --> 00:53:16.120
so he didn't have roots there.

00:53:16.120 --> 00:53:20.587
But that lynching continued
to shape the racial politics

00:53:23.330 --> 00:53:27.400
and society in the town
for over 100 years.

00:53:27.400 --> 00:53:30.570
And so there are these competing accounts.

00:53:30.570 --> 00:53:31.940
Was it justice in action,

00:53:31.940 --> 00:53:34.973
did he really murder
Effie Greer Henderson,

00:53:36.200 --> 00:53:39.900
or was he just a proxy, and
was it really somebody else,

00:53:39.900 --> 00:53:41.790
and was it actually a brutal,

00:53:41.790 --> 00:53:43.457
brutal immoral act of mob violence?

00:53:43.457 --> 00:53:45.930
And so those tensions
coalesce in the town,

00:53:45.930 --> 00:53:47.610
and they don't fall along racial lines.

00:53:47.610 --> 00:53:49.660
And so that was something interesting

00:53:49.660 --> 00:53:52.420
that I wanted to point out in the book.

00:53:52.420 --> 00:53:55.750
That there are competing memories,

00:53:55.750 --> 00:53:57.340
and they do different work.

00:53:57.340 --> 00:54:01.440
And so the circulation of
an account of the lynching

00:54:01.440 --> 00:54:04.870
by civil rights activists in
the 60s and 70s for example,

00:54:04.870 --> 00:54:07.537
animated a particular kind of organizing.

00:54:07.537 --> 00:54:10.070
That young Chicanos and Chicanas

00:54:10.070 --> 00:54:13.430
were imagining themselves as responding to

00:54:13.430 --> 00:54:16.860
long histories of violence
and discrimination.

00:54:16.860 --> 00:54:19.390
And so sharing that
story helped to mobilize

00:54:19.390 --> 00:54:22.780
a certain kind of
awakening or consciousness.

00:54:22.780 --> 00:54:25.540
And in another context,

00:54:25.540 --> 00:54:29.700
sharing that account warned
a Mexican sheep shearer

00:54:29.700 --> 00:54:32.180
not to have relations
with white women, right.

00:54:32.180 --> 00:54:34.710
That even proximity to
white women is dangerous.

00:54:34.710 --> 00:54:36.080
And so I was interested in what kind of,

00:54:36.080 --> 00:54:38.240
like when did people
decide to share this story,

00:54:38.240 --> 00:54:41.040
and what was the lessons
that were being conveyed.

00:54:41.040 --> 00:54:42.900
But also, how is it that 100 years later

00:54:42.900 --> 00:54:45.240
people were still remembering this event?

00:54:45.240 --> 00:54:48.950
But then I also as a historian,

00:54:48.950 --> 00:54:52.483
for people who think that
memory is not serious work,

00:54:54.300 --> 00:54:56.200
interested in using oral histories

00:54:56.200 --> 00:54:59.740
for what they expose in archives.

00:54:59.740 --> 00:55:03.050
And so for the Porvenir
massacre, for example,

00:55:03.050 --> 00:55:05.980
if I hadn't conducted the oral
history with the Alvarados,

00:55:05.980 --> 00:55:07.890
I would not have found the court case

00:55:07.890 --> 00:55:10.920
that was filed by the widows in 1926.

00:55:10.920 --> 00:55:14.080
And so 12 survivors of that massacre

00:55:14.080 --> 00:55:15.910
filed a claim against the US government,

00:55:15.910 --> 00:55:20.910
which led me to other
cases of Mexican nationals

00:55:21.550 --> 00:55:22.950
that had filed cases

00:55:22.950 --> 00:55:25.640
against the US government
seeking indemnities.

00:55:25.640 --> 00:55:28.350
And so it led me to a legal archive

00:55:28.350 --> 00:55:30.620
that historians hadn't found.

00:55:30.620 --> 00:55:32.430
And so, so part of the work,

00:55:32.430 --> 00:55:35.180
so there's the study of memory itself,

00:55:35.180 --> 00:55:37.410
and then there's the use of oral histories

00:55:37.410 --> 00:55:42.410
that point you to archives
that haven't been analyzed.

00:55:42.920 --> 00:55:44.400
And again, thinking about the work

00:55:44.400 --> 00:55:45.660
of Norma Longoria Rodriguez

00:55:45.660 --> 00:55:48.240
to prompt me to ask different questions.

00:55:48.240 --> 00:55:49.757
And so I think that the work,

00:55:49.757 --> 00:55:52.510
and the importance of
oral history is multiple,

00:55:52.510 --> 00:55:53.990
and it just takes,

00:55:53.990 --> 00:55:56.810
if you pull together the right committee

00:55:56.810 --> 00:55:59.560
that understand the
politics of the questions,

00:55:59.560 --> 00:56:02.463
so I was taking classes with Alicia,

00:56:03.530 --> 00:56:07.900
who was thinking about how is
it that people seek justice

00:56:08.800 --> 00:56:11.453
in the face of negligence by the state.

00:56:14.489 --> 00:56:16.620
I was thinking about (speaks
foreign language) for example

00:56:17.640 --> 00:56:19.400
and that what is the work
that women were doing

00:56:19.400 --> 00:56:21.890
just showing up publicly to mourn.

00:56:21.890 --> 00:56:26.120
And so that helped me ask
new questions as well.

00:56:26.120 --> 00:56:27.868
But it also helped me engage

00:56:27.868 --> 00:56:29.810
just questions of what does it mean

00:56:29.810 --> 00:56:32.130
to take on the question of what is loss.

00:56:32.130 --> 00:56:34.550
What does it mean to analyze loss?

00:56:34.550 --> 00:56:37.800
And so I really was
working with historians

00:56:37.800 --> 00:56:39.900
and people in cultural studies,

00:56:39.900 --> 00:56:41.900
and building this
interdisciplinary project,

00:56:41.900 --> 00:56:44.882
but I had a committee that understand

00:56:44.882 --> 00:56:48.527
the politics of the work and
the urgency of the questions.

00:56:48.527 --> 00:56:51.110
And so I think part of it
is finding the right methods

00:56:51.110 --> 00:56:52.260
and articulating the methods

00:56:52.260 --> 00:56:55.610
that help you answer the
questions that feel urgent to you.

00:56:55.610 --> 00:56:57.780
And sometimes it maybe
just means pulling together

00:56:57.780 --> 00:56:59.840
the right group of people.

00:56:59.840 --> 00:57:01.640
Joanne Meyerowitz, from what I know,

00:57:01.640 --> 00:57:04.580
unless she's had this
whole other life before,

00:57:04.580 --> 00:57:06.660
wasn't studying the borderlands,

00:57:06.660 --> 00:57:09.610
but she could understand
having a feminist praxis

00:57:09.610 --> 00:57:11.730
for our doing archival work.

00:57:11.730 --> 00:57:14.050
And so I think it's,

00:57:14.050 --> 00:57:16.750
I had this really excellent
synergy with my committee.

00:57:18.320 --> 00:57:19.870
But it's an important question.

00:57:27.476 --> 00:57:28.780
- [David] First of all,
thank you Professor Martinez.

00:57:28.780 --> 00:57:30.660
My name is David, and I'm
actually from Brownsville,

00:57:30.660 --> 00:57:33.310
so this is all very
relevant to my upbringing.

00:57:33.310 --> 00:57:35.900
But I'm aware of how Brownsville

00:57:35.900 --> 00:57:38.250
and South Texas are now
as demographic wise,

00:57:38.250 --> 00:57:40.693
but what does Brownsville
and South Texas look like

00:57:40.693 --> 00:57:43.210
in 1910 to 1920 when this
violence is happening?

00:57:43.210 --> 00:57:45.190
What's sort of the distribution
of the demographics?

00:57:45.190 --> 00:57:50.190
Is it, I suppose what
percentage of Mexican immigrants

00:57:50.370 --> 00:57:52.280
are there living in these areas?

00:57:52.280 --> 00:57:54.490
- Oh the question of percentages.

00:57:54.490 --> 00:57:56.090
I'll have to send you the numbers of that,

00:57:56.090 --> 00:57:57.480
because I don't have it offhand,

00:57:57.480 --> 00:58:02.160
but in terms of the, the
demographics are changing.

00:58:02.160 --> 00:58:04.943
So you really have by 1904,

00:58:06.570 --> 00:58:08.500
the railroad arrives in Brownsville,

00:58:08.500 --> 00:58:11.210
and it brings with it Anglo migrants,

00:58:11.210 --> 00:58:13.460
you know Anglos who are
coming from the south

00:58:13.460 --> 00:58:14.480
and who are coming from the north

00:58:14.480 --> 00:58:16.870
who are bringing in new ideas

00:58:16.870 --> 00:58:19.620
about race and racial hierarchies.

00:58:19.620 --> 00:58:21.310
And so there had been a longer effort

00:58:21.310 --> 00:58:26.310
to remove a Mexican land-owning community,

00:58:28.780 --> 00:58:32.690
but also Mexican residents,
many who were American citizens

00:58:32.690 --> 00:58:36.150
who were also engaged in
politics and in voting,

00:58:36.150 --> 00:58:39.280
who were engaged in
educating their children.

00:58:39.280 --> 00:58:41.780
And so you have this,
what was introduced as,

00:58:41.780 --> 00:58:43.950
historians called the Juan Crow,

00:58:43.950 --> 00:58:45.350
bringing Juan Crow laws

00:58:45.350 --> 00:58:47.260
of racial segregation to South Texas.

00:58:47.260 --> 00:58:51.490
And so that included segregating
children from schools,

00:58:51.490 --> 00:58:54.583
prohibiting interracial marriages,

00:58:58.670 --> 00:59:02.500
banning ethnic Mexicans from
participating in juries,

00:59:02.500 --> 00:59:06.840
and also using racial intimidation
for disenfranchisement.

00:59:06.840 --> 00:59:08.740
Also passing things like poll taxes.

00:59:08.740 --> 00:59:10.750
And so there was this effort really

00:59:10.750 --> 00:59:13.850
to create this new racial hierarchy.

00:59:13.850 --> 00:59:18.147
And when laws and implementing
laws weren't as effective,

00:59:19.420 --> 00:59:21.590
especially in trying
to displace landowners,

00:59:21.590 --> 00:59:23.620
then racial violence was used.

00:59:23.620 --> 00:59:27.460
And so the common phrase in this era

00:59:27.460 --> 00:59:30.680
was you don't buy from the
husband you buy from the widow.

00:59:30.680 --> 00:59:35.010
So you murder the husband and
then you intimidate the widow,

00:59:35.010 --> 00:59:37.270
and you then take land that way.

00:59:37.270 --> 00:59:38.973
But there's, what's happening

00:59:40.070 --> 00:59:43.110
in terms of the shifting economy

00:59:43.110 --> 00:59:46.750
is that this migration of Anglos

00:59:46.750 --> 00:59:49.100
changes the way that land is used.

00:59:49.100 --> 00:59:53.670
And so Texas changes from a
primarily ranching economy

00:59:53.670 --> 00:59:56.400
in South Texas to a farming economy.

00:59:56.400 --> 00:59:59.590
So it's also been described
by David Montejano

00:59:59.590 --> 01:00:02.020
as the farm colonization of the Southwest.

01:00:02.020 --> 01:00:07.020
And so you see that the
change in the use of land,

01:00:07.280 --> 01:00:10.000
but also Mexicans move from being

01:00:12.880 --> 01:00:14.540
members of the landed elite

01:00:14.540 --> 01:00:17.510
to primarily being wage laborers.

01:00:17.510 --> 01:00:20.430
And so it's a shifting of
the racial hierarchies.

01:00:20.430 --> 01:00:21.980
Now of course it's important to remember

01:00:21.980 --> 01:00:25.520
that this is, there are layered
histories of colonization.

01:00:25.520 --> 01:00:28.360
So Antonio Longoria, for
example, has his land,

01:00:28.360 --> 01:00:30.800
can be traced back to a
Spanish land grant, right.

01:00:30.800 --> 01:00:35.300
So there are histories
of colonization that,

01:00:35.300 --> 01:00:37.200
and the patterns of policing

01:00:37.200 --> 01:00:39.663
that targeted different
racial and ethnic groups.

01:00:40.610 --> 01:00:43.240
Some of the Texas Rangers
that are in this hall of fame

01:00:43.240 --> 01:00:47.019
are celebrated for participating
in massacre and genocide

01:00:47.019 --> 01:00:49.710
of indigenous people in Texas.

01:00:49.710 --> 01:00:52.930
Some of them are also celebrated
for being slave catchers.

01:00:52.930 --> 01:00:54.390
I mean, I'm not exaggerating,

01:00:54.390 --> 01:00:56.150
they describe people as slave catchers,

01:00:56.150 --> 01:00:58.540
and promote that in their bios

01:00:58.540 --> 01:00:59.810
of some of these Texas Rangers.

01:00:59.810 --> 01:01:02.270
And so it is a place

01:01:02.270 --> 01:01:06.340
where the history of
genocidal colonization

01:01:06.340 --> 01:01:07.610
and the history of slavery

01:01:07.610 --> 01:01:09.650
and the history of US
empire and nation building

01:01:09.650 --> 01:01:12.190
are all colliding, and in
the early 20th century,

01:01:12.190 --> 01:01:16.710
you see these tensions being
targeted at ethnic Mexicans.

01:01:16.710 --> 01:01:17.640
It's a complicated era,

01:01:17.640 --> 01:01:20.730
but the numbers, I'll
have to send them to you.

01:01:20.730 --> 01:01:21.563
- [David] Thank you.

01:01:26.030 --> 01:01:27.655
- [Fernando] Hello, thank you so much.

01:01:27.655 --> 01:01:29.570
My name is Fernando.

01:01:29.570 --> 01:01:30.560
I loved your book.

01:01:30.560 --> 01:01:34.170
Could you please speak to
the section on Dairy Queen?

01:01:34.170 --> 01:01:35.530
I just thought it was so phenomenal

01:01:35.530 --> 01:01:38.250
how it's such a public space,

01:01:38.250 --> 01:01:41.120
and yet like the way that
you unpacked the history,

01:01:41.120 --> 01:01:43.903
and how photographs there
have normalized violence,

01:01:45.040 --> 01:01:47.500
just what that experience was for you

01:01:47.500 --> 01:01:50.840
seeing that as you were
working through this project,

01:01:50.840 --> 01:01:52.610
as you were writing it as well.

01:01:52.610 --> 01:01:55.287
And then also a broader
question just about capitalism

01:01:55.287 --> 01:01:59.440
and how it influenced, like is this,

01:01:59.440 --> 01:02:01.160
could it be conceptualized this work

01:02:01.160 --> 01:02:02.840
as like a history of capitalism

01:02:02.840 --> 01:02:07.500
as postcards are printed

01:02:07.500 --> 01:02:11.690
with people dying or
dead on the postcards.

01:02:11.690 --> 01:02:14.290
How does violence become

01:02:14.290 --> 01:02:18.383
a form in which capitalism
grows or is perpetuated?

01:02:19.420 --> 01:02:20.790
- Okay, those are great questions.

01:02:20.790 --> 01:02:23.260
Well, I mean to talk about
the Dairy Queen briefly,

01:02:23.260 --> 01:02:25.870
you know it was an eerie experience,

01:02:25.870 --> 01:02:29.083
so I was driving home to
see my family in Uvalde

01:02:29.083 --> 01:02:30.940
because I had been doing archival work,

01:02:30.940 --> 01:02:32.230
and then I stopped in a Dairy Queen,

01:02:32.230 --> 01:02:33.500
which is you know, on these road trips,

01:02:33.500 --> 01:02:35.480
you need to go to the bathroom,
you need some iced tea,

01:02:35.480 --> 01:02:40.140
and so Sabinal is not too,
you know maybe 20 miles

01:02:40.140 --> 01:02:42.620
from where my parents live in Uvalde,

01:02:42.620 --> 01:02:45.930
but anyhow, so there was a Texas Ranger,

01:02:45.930 --> 01:02:48.830
a photographic exhibit
honoring the Texas Rangers.

01:02:48.830 --> 01:02:53.430
And so it included
photographs of a lynching.

01:02:53.430 --> 01:02:56.180
And so it was this moment,

01:02:56.180 --> 01:02:58.590
I had been conducting archival work

01:02:58.590 --> 01:03:00.770
on the history of anti-Mexican violence,

01:03:00.770 --> 01:03:03.930
and this was one of those
other moments, you know.

01:03:03.930 --> 01:03:07.400
Talking to Norma Longoria
Rodriguez and having pie with her

01:03:07.400 --> 01:03:09.250
was one of these moments.

01:03:09.250 --> 01:03:10.980
Talking to the Alvarados in Uvalde

01:03:10.980 --> 01:03:12.610
about the Porvenir massacre,

01:03:12.610 --> 01:03:15.260
and hearing about the conversations

01:03:15.260 --> 01:03:17.880
where they had talked to the local county

01:03:17.880 --> 01:03:20.840
about having historical
marker, and been dismissed.

01:03:20.840 --> 01:03:24.870
Or traveling to archives
and seeing archivists

01:03:24.870 --> 01:03:27.950
pull documents out of the
folders that they requested.

01:03:27.950 --> 01:03:28.930
There were these other moments

01:03:28.930 --> 01:03:32.300
where I thought that the
need to study oral history

01:03:32.300 --> 01:03:34.220
is also about studying the politics

01:03:34.220 --> 01:03:37.890
of how history is produced,
and what who it's serving,

01:03:37.890 --> 01:03:41.240
and who is committed to
protecting a version of history.

01:03:41.240 --> 01:03:45.473
How is power, memory, and
history all coalescing.

01:03:46.611 --> 01:03:51.410
So this Dairy Queen exhibit
with these lynching photographs

01:03:51.410 --> 01:03:53.990
for me is a really profound example

01:03:53.990 --> 01:03:56.780
of the kind of racial intimidation

01:03:56.780 --> 01:03:58.740
that people are exposed to.

01:03:58.740 --> 01:04:03.090
So for people who identify with
victims of racial violence,

01:04:03.090 --> 01:04:04.730
who say that might look like me,

01:04:04.730 --> 01:04:06.550
or that reminds me of
somebody from my family

01:04:06.550 --> 01:04:08.520
who was treated that way,

01:04:08.520 --> 01:04:09.760
that is the kind of image

01:04:09.760 --> 01:04:13.800
that reminds you of what your place is.

01:04:13.800 --> 01:04:16.080
But they're similar, it's
not a lynching photograph,

01:04:16.080 --> 01:04:18.740
but also you know the
the wrought iron signs

01:04:18.740 --> 01:04:21.160
across the state you know
it's like Texas kitsch,

01:04:21.160 --> 01:04:22.590
you know we don't call 9-1-1,

01:04:22.590 --> 01:04:26.140
and it's got the Colt
44 revolver, you know.

01:04:26.140 --> 01:04:29.460
It's like metalwork, and
people put them on their homes,

01:04:29.460 --> 01:04:33.200
and shops have posters that say

01:04:33.200 --> 01:04:36.330
we don't call the police,
we prosecute shoplifters,

01:04:36.330 --> 01:04:41.270
and it's got people on a
scaffold before being hanged.

01:04:41.270 --> 01:04:44.740
And so these kinds of images,

01:04:44.740 --> 01:04:45.940
I was really struck by

01:04:45.940 --> 01:04:48.470
not only the way that it had been curated,

01:04:48.470 --> 01:04:49.730
because there were no captions,

01:04:49.730 --> 01:04:52.690
you didn't get the sense of
this could have been in Sabinal,

01:04:52.690 --> 01:04:53.523
it could have been anywhere,

01:04:53.523 --> 01:04:55.360
we didn't know who the victims were,

01:04:55.360 --> 01:04:58.220
but the way the kind of
work that it was doing,

01:04:58.220 --> 01:05:00.180
and talking to the
women that worked there,

01:05:00.180 --> 01:05:02.110
saying things like I don't
like to take out the trash

01:05:02.110 --> 01:05:04.350
because I don't want to
look at those photographs.

01:05:04.350 --> 01:05:06.760
But also that the person
who curated the exhibit

01:05:06.760 --> 01:05:09.530
was so happy to talk about
the Texas Rangers, right.

01:05:09.530 --> 01:05:14.250
So he was part of creating
reenactment groups,

01:05:14.250 --> 01:05:18.123
and celebrating this era of
Texas Rangers in particular,

01:05:19.580 --> 01:05:23.210
from like the 1880s through the 19-teens.

01:05:23.210 --> 01:05:25.780
And so it was also then an experience

01:05:25.780 --> 01:05:30.230
to try to give space for thinking through

01:05:31.260 --> 01:05:34.210
what is the interest in somebody
in these reenactment groups

01:05:34.210 --> 01:05:36.390
in creating these the celebration,

01:05:36.390 --> 01:05:39.050
and being a part of participating

01:05:39.050 --> 01:05:40.810
in vernacular history making.

01:05:40.810 --> 01:05:43.160
You know, not to dismantle
these mainstream narratives

01:05:43.160 --> 01:05:44.460
but to reinforce them.

01:05:44.460 --> 01:05:46.010
And especially in rural communities

01:05:46.010 --> 01:05:49.690
where people don't have access
to cultural institutions.

01:05:49.690 --> 01:05:52.320
The Dairy Queen is what you do.

01:05:52.320 --> 01:05:54.080
It's where old men drink coffee.

01:05:54.080 --> 01:05:55.440
And so I was really interested

01:05:55.440 --> 01:05:57.990
in how these politics play out in ways.

01:05:57.990 --> 01:06:01.270
I mean, it's racism in plain sight, right.

01:06:01.270 --> 01:06:02.730
So people think about monuments,

01:06:02.730 --> 01:06:05.020
Confederate monuments coming down,

01:06:05.020 --> 01:06:08.010
but this is another history
that is just celebrate

01:06:08.010 --> 01:06:11.923
without because most people
don't know this history,

01:06:12.820 --> 01:06:16.360
or they do and they really do
think that this is progress.

01:06:16.360 --> 01:06:18.210
They really do think that Native Americans

01:06:18.210 --> 01:06:19.910
in this era were savage.

01:06:19.910 --> 01:06:23.960
And so for them this is,
they're honoring Texans

01:06:23.960 --> 01:06:26.510
that helped to build the state.

01:06:26.510 --> 01:06:27.920
So that's interesting.

01:06:27.920 --> 01:06:29.420
You had a second question though.

01:06:29.420 --> 01:06:32.840
- [Fernando] Yeah, about like,
like if can your book be told

01:06:32.840 --> 01:06:36.573
like in, like a history
of capitalism in Texas.

01:06:37.464 --> 01:06:38.297
- I think so.

01:06:38.297 --> 01:06:39.130
I mean, certainly if you,

01:06:39.130 --> 01:06:40.760
I mean if you're thinking
about the commodification of,

01:06:40.760 --> 01:06:43.730
I mean the ways in which
racial violence was also used

01:06:43.730 --> 01:06:48.210
to suppress union
organizing, labor organizing,

01:06:48.210 --> 01:06:51.660
and also to intimidate a
vulnerable labor force, certainly.

01:06:51.660 --> 01:06:53.630
I mean, there's a long
history of Texas Rangers

01:06:53.630 --> 01:06:56.040
and border patrol being used

01:06:56.040 --> 01:07:00.960
to suppress efforts for labor rights,

01:07:00.960 --> 01:07:05.180
or also just to you know
deport laborers before payday.

01:07:05.180 --> 01:07:09.740
So there's, the history of
policing is deeply interconnected

01:07:09.740 --> 01:07:11.463
with the exploitation of labor,

01:07:12.360 --> 01:07:15.330
and the recruitment of vulnerable labor,

01:07:15.330 --> 01:07:17.321
and then the exploitation of that labor.

01:07:17.321 --> 01:07:21.920
But in the terms of the
commodification of the postcards,

01:07:21.920 --> 01:07:24.680
certainly, it's a, there's a history,

01:07:24.680 --> 01:07:27.860
you know, the history of
photography in this era.

01:07:27.860 --> 01:07:31.720
There's the use of, I
describe it as the convergence

01:07:31.720 --> 01:07:34.760
of wartime photography,

01:07:34.760 --> 01:07:36.540
the photographers that
are taking photographs

01:07:36.540 --> 01:07:38.340
of the Mexican Revolution,

01:07:38.340 --> 01:07:42.966
but also of the development of this,

01:07:42.966 --> 01:07:46.610
the sell of these postcards as souvenirs.

01:07:46.610 --> 01:07:50.670
So it's a way that you
create not only a market

01:07:50.670 --> 01:07:55.280
that celebrates this kind of
dehumanization in photography,

01:07:55.280 --> 01:07:57.140
but you also see the wider circulation

01:07:57.140 --> 01:07:58.850
of the criminalization of Mexicans.

01:07:58.850 --> 01:08:01.520
And so you see the figure
of the Mexican bandit

01:08:03.110 --> 01:08:04.530
taking root in Texas,

01:08:04.530 --> 01:08:07.960
but then also change,
creating a national figure

01:08:07.960 --> 01:08:11.600
of the menacing Mexican that
we are still living with today.

01:08:11.600 --> 01:08:13.560
So this was not just the postcards,

01:08:13.560 --> 01:08:15.340
but also the political cartoons,

01:08:15.340 --> 01:08:17.210
and in another version,

01:08:17.210 --> 01:08:21.040
when I've given talks to
bigger public audiences,

01:08:21.040 --> 01:08:23.560
I show some of those political cartoons.

01:08:23.560 --> 01:08:25.964
It's this moment when you
see the crystallization

01:08:25.964 --> 01:08:30.300
of Mexicans as bandit,
as foreigner, as threat

01:08:31.290 --> 01:08:32.523
being cemented.

01:08:33.842 --> 01:08:36.060
- [Woman] We have time
for one more question.

01:08:36.060 --> 01:08:37.740
- Oh there's two, so maybe you both ask,

01:08:37.740 --> 01:08:40.253
and then and then I'll
combine them really quickly.

01:08:44.210 --> 01:08:45.310
- [Larissa] Thank you.

01:08:46.390 --> 01:08:49.210
Okay, so my name is Larissa.

01:08:49.210 --> 01:08:50.400
Thank you for your talk.

01:08:50.400 --> 01:08:52.973
I mean I think this is
really important work.

01:08:54.390 --> 01:08:58.220
But yeah so I guess my question
is where do we go from here?

01:08:58.220 --> 01:09:01.390
Like I think it's really
interesting that you chose Texas,

01:09:01.390 --> 01:09:04.800
and then like this, I just
think it's sort of ironic

01:09:04.800 --> 01:09:07.680
considering Texas is also like,

01:09:07.680 --> 01:09:11.290
like produces text books,
and it's like a lot of,

01:09:11.290 --> 01:09:13.400
yeah it's like missing
so much information.

01:09:13.400 --> 01:09:15.570
And so I guess, I don't know,

01:09:15.570 --> 01:09:18.820
what role do you think
that books like yours

01:09:18.820 --> 01:09:22.040
can play in teaching
more of the community,

01:09:22.040 --> 01:09:24.660
and how do we make those
things also accessible?

01:09:24.660 --> 01:09:26.130
Because I know a lot of times

01:09:26.130 --> 01:09:31.130
academic papers are written
for people in academia,

01:09:31.460 --> 01:09:33.440
and so it's, yeah, like
sometimes it's really hard

01:09:33.440 --> 01:09:36.260
to then go back to like for
example rural communities,

01:09:36.260 --> 01:09:37.420
and try to just be like,

01:09:37.420 --> 01:09:39.930
no actually, all that
they taught you was wrong.

01:09:39.930 --> 01:09:43.350
So how can you really
have engaging dialogue

01:09:43.350 --> 01:09:46.250
where people can start seeing,

01:09:46.250 --> 01:09:48.630
yeah, like seeing this without also,

01:09:48.630 --> 01:09:50.310
I mean the history is really violent,

01:09:50.310 --> 01:09:52.160
and I can, yeah, and also even like

01:09:52.160 --> 01:09:56.310
how do you begin teaching
children, like at what ages,

01:09:56.310 --> 01:09:57.260
but I don't know.

01:09:57.260 --> 01:09:59.360
- Yeah, these are really
important questions,

01:09:59.360 --> 01:10:00.290
and these are some of the questions

01:10:00.290 --> 01:10:02.080
that I don't have all the answers for.

01:10:02.080 --> 01:10:04.550
But you know, one of the
things for Refusing to Forget

01:10:04.550 --> 01:10:07.150
that I started to do, you know,

01:10:07.150 --> 01:10:09.230
one of the things was to build a,

01:10:09.230 --> 01:10:11.610
yeah quickly ask your question.

01:10:11.610 --> 01:10:13.070
We'll talk at dinner.

01:10:13.070 --> 01:10:14.300
Okay, we have time, okay.

01:10:14.300 --> 01:10:15.180
So really quickly though.

01:10:15.180 --> 01:10:17.730
But it was to write a
part of the public work

01:10:17.730 --> 01:10:21.333
for Refusing to Forget, as I
brought students on at Brown,

01:10:22.350 --> 01:10:25.690
a group of graduate students
and undergraduate students

01:10:25.690 --> 01:10:27.170
and we wrote a curriculum,

01:10:27.170 --> 01:10:29.570
four-day curriculum on Jovita Idar.

01:10:29.570 --> 01:10:32.010
So again, how do you
tell this brutal history

01:10:32.010 --> 01:10:33.420
of racial violence to children,

01:10:33.420 --> 01:10:35.523
which is not my, that's not my skill set.

01:10:36.510 --> 01:10:38.760
But so we developed this lesson
plan, and it can be modular

01:10:38.760 --> 01:10:42.260
so that teachers can use
different different parts of it,

01:10:42.260 --> 01:10:44.210
but it gives a history
of this racial violence,

01:10:44.210 --> 01:10:46.210
a history of the Texas
Rangers, and the brutality,

01:10:46.210 --> 01:10:48.460
but it also gives the
history of Jovita Idar,

01:10:49.500 --> 01:10:51.830
who not only was a journalist

01:10:51.830 --> 01:10:53.870
who literally stood in
front of the Texas Rangers

01:10:53.870 --> 01:10:57.150
to try to keep them from
burning down a printing press,

01:10:57.150 --> 01:10:58.440
they eventually burned it down,

01:10:58.440 --> 01:11:02.120
but before they did, she also
wrote these newspaper articles

01:11:02.120 --> 01:11:03.670
documenting the atrocities.

01:11:03.670 --> 01:11:06.030
She also organized for women's suffrage,

01:11:06.030 --> 01:11:07.660
she also organized for access

01:11:07.660 --> 01:11:10.133
to public schools by
these Mexican children,

01:11:11.474 --> 01:11:14.150
and she also helped build

01:11:14.150 --> 01:11:18.512
the first cross-border
civil rights conference

01:11:18.512 --> 01:11:20.402
(speaks foreign language)

01:11:20.402 --> 01:11:21.580
And she had, and she organized

01:11:21.580 --> 01:11:23.540
a women's conference alongside it.

01:11:23.540 --> 01:11:25.640
And so it was this conference

01:11:25.640 --> 01:11:28.520
that invited people from
both sides of the border,

01:11:28.520 --> 01:11:30.920
because in this era there
was not really a border,

01:11:30.920 --> 01:11:33.200
people moved fluidly back and forth.

01:11:33.200 --> 01:11:35.300
And so she imagined civil rights

01:11:35.300 --> 01:11:37.520
organizing in a fight for social justice

01:11:37.520 --> 01:11:41.080
having to be a cross-border coalition.

01:11:41.080 --> 01:11:45.720
And so bringing these sort
of radical calls for justice,

01:11:45.720 --> 01:11:47.390
bringing a figure like Jovita Idar

01:11:47.390 --> 01:11:50.500
to a public audience, to an
elementary school audience,

01:11:50.500 --> 01:11:53.090
to me was a really
captivating opportunity.

01:11:53.090 --> 01:11:55.160
And it's something,

01:11:55.160 --> 01:11:58.470
we matched it up so it fulfills the TEKS,

01:11:58.470 --> 01:12:00.850
knowing that every student
in Texas in public schools

01:12:00.850 --> 01:12:03.610
has to take Texas history in K through 12,

01:12:03.610 --> 01:12:04.930
and if they're a university student,

01:12:04.930 --> 01:12:06.300
they have to take it again,

01:12:06.300 --> 01:12:08.553
that's a big group of students.

01:12:09.700 --> 01:12:11.440
So making it fulfill the requirements

01:12:11.440 --> 01:12:12.273
that a teacher would need

01:12:12.273 --> 01:12:14.320
to teach that in a Texas history class.

01:12:14.320 --> 01:12:18.420
Now, you know my parents
walked out of schools in 1970

01:12:18.420 --> 01:12:21.010
to protest discrimination
and segregation in schools,

01:12:21.010 --> 01:12:22.160
and one of the things that they demanded

01:12:22.160 --> 01:12:24.760
was Mexican-American studies
be taught in public schools,

01:12:24.760 --> 01:12:29.750
and finally in 2019,
it's going to be offered

01:12:29.750 --> 01:12:31.980
by teachers and school
districts that support it,

01:12:31.980 --> 01:12:33.850
but students can get state credit

01:12:33.850 --> 01:12:35.410
for taking Mexican-American studies.

01:12:35.410 --> 01:12:37.480
And so I'm part of a group of historians

01:12:37.480 --> 01:12:41.030
that are just hustling to try
to help develop curriculum.

01:12:41.030 --> 01:12:43.380
And so Arlinda Valencia,

01:12:43.380 --> 01:12:45.939
who is a descendant of
the Porvenir massacre,

01:12:45.939 --> 01:12:49.580
has written some lesson plans
about the Porvenir massacre,

01:12:49.580 --> 01:12:51.640
and so we are working really actively

01:12:51.640 --> 01:12:55.060
to figure out how you
reach a younger audience.

01:12:55.060 --> 01:12:57.350
I paired up with Professor Chris Carmona,

01:12:57.350 --> 01:12:59.220
who just wrote a book called El Rinche.

01:12:59.220 --> 01:13:04.200
It's a piece of fiction about this era,

01:13:04.200 --> 01:13:06.589
but it's for young adults.

01:13:06.589 --> 01:13:10.910
So there's, that is an active effort.

01:13:10.910 --> 01:13:14.630
But I think that having the
historical marker is one thing,

01:13:14.630 --> 01:13:15.930
having the book is one thing,

01:13:15.930 --> 01:13:19.650
but then how you actually
activate the history

01:13:19.650 --> 01:13:22.360
to shape and change public understandings

01:13:22.360 --> 01:13:24.660
of the border and of immigration.

01:13:24.660 --> 01:13:26.180
You know, my sister is an artist,

01:13:26.180 --> 01:13:27.480
and she says the border,

01:13:27.480 --> 01:13:29.240
people know the border
as a place of horror,

01:13:29.240 --> 01:13:31.433
but it's, she knows it as a place of hope.

01:13:32.380 --> 01:13:34.063
So we need to get back there.

01:13:36.920 --> 01:13:38.790
- [Woman] Thank you, I like the
way you answer the question.

01:13:38.790 --> 01:13:42.567
I was gonna about the
(mumbles) oral history,

01:13:42.567 --> 01:13:45.720
and that role that oral history allows

01:13:45.720 --> 01:13:49.840
academics to not only
produce academic research,

01:13:49.840 --> 01:13:52.890
but also leading to public history,

01:13:52.890 --> 01:13:55.270
and how that takes place
for you as a historian.

01:13:55.270 --> 01:13:58.560
- Yeah, I mean, I think part
of it is that there's just,

01:13:58.560 --> 01:14:02.050
for me, I think that as a historian

01:14:02.050 --> 01:14:05.550
or people who study
histories of racial violence

01:14:05.550 --> 01:14:07.780
that once you have
learned these histories,

01:14:07.780 --> 01:14:09.180
and once you are starting to help

01:14:09.180 --> 01:14:13.680
document these histories for
publication, for academic work,

01:14:13.680 --> 01:14:15.320
that we have a moral obligation

01:14:15.320 --> 01:14:18.540
to make sure that it's
available to a wider public.

01:14:18.540 --> 01:14:22.120
And so and I also felt a moral obligation

01:14:22.120 --> 01:14:23.780
for these families who had been working

01:14:23.780 --> 01:14:27.940
to try to make these histories
public for over 100 years

01:14:27.940 --> 01:14:31.760
to leverage my PhD to
do some of that work.

01:14:31.760 --> 01:14:34.930
So you know, and we've received

01:14:34.930 --> 01:14:38.130
a considerable amount of
resistance most recently.

01:14:38.130 --> 01:14:40.090
And I am still concerned.

01:14:40.090 --> 01:14:43.020
There's still a lot of good
work that public history can do,

01:14:43.020 --> 01:14:44.810
and I'm very hopeful,

01:14:44.810 --> 01:14:49.810
but I also now see that
there is possibility

01:14:50.760 --> 01:14:53.360
for some of these efforts to create harm.

01:14:53.360 --> 01:14:56.210
And so for a descendant
of the Porvenir massacre

01:14:56.210 --> 01:14:59.980
to read you know local members of a county

01:14:59.980 --> 01:15:04.630
describe their family members
as bandits, and as rapists,

01:15:04.630 --> 01:15:09.630
and to say awful things
in public correspondence,

01:15:10.480 --> 01:15:13.943
or to describe them as militant Hispanics,

01:15:15.910 --> 01:15:19.473
the progress that we
have made is invaluable.

01:15:20.340 --> 01:15:22.690
But again, it's about recognizing

01:15:22.690 --> 01:15:24.850
that the urgency for
making this history public

01:15:24.850 --> 01:15:27.963
is because we are living
in a violent context.

01:15:28.890 --> 01:15:30.100
We are living in another era

01:15:30.100 --> 01:15:31.620
where troops are being sent to the border,

01:15:31.620 --> 01:15:33.000
where immigrants are being,

01:15:33.000 --> 01:15:35.120
where people who are seeking refuge,

01:15:35.120 --> 01:15:37.003
asylum seekers, are being imprisoned,

01:15:37.885 --> 01:15:42.480
and where there is a rise

01:15:42.480 --> 01:15:45.030
of white supremacy and racial hate.

01:15:45.030 --> 01:15:49.450
And so in this era, we have
had some very good luck

01:15:49.450 --> 01:15:51.850
finding partners and allies in the state

01:15:51.850 --> 01:15:53.850
to help have a truthful accounting

01:15:53.850 --> 01:15:55.660
and to show real leadership,

01:15:55.660 --> 01:15:57.710
but I'm also seeing that now,

01:15:57.710 --> 01:16:00.230
as some of these events
have become more public,

01:16:00.230 --> 01:16:02.000
and we've received more attention,

01:16:02.000 --> 01:16:05.737
that there is now an active
effort to stop the progress.

01:16:08.660 --> 01:16:10.360
And so in some cases,

01:16:10.360 --> 01:16:14.913
it has exposed a a
troubling part of Texas.

01:16:17.360 --> 01:16:18.940
But I'm more determined than ever.

01:16:18.940 --> 01:16:21.113
So the book is out.

01:16:22.640 --> 01:16:24.360
There's nothing they can do about that.

01:16:24.360 --> 01:16:27.660
The marker is up, (laughs)
nothing they can do about that.

01:16:27.660 --> 01:16:30.653
So you know determined to
keep making more progress.

01:16:32.110 --> 01:16:33.639
- [Woman] Please join me
in thanking Professor.

01:16:33.639 --> 01:16:36.806
(audience applauding)

01:16:37.928 --> 01:16:40.947
- Thank you.

01:16:43.289 --> 01:16:46.622
(calm percussive music)

